{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content SPEC.md; Get-Content sim.py; Get-Content bayes.py; Get-Content latent_filter.py; Get-Content e12_unlabelled_head.py; Get-Content e13_rungs_over_time.py; Get-Content figures/e13_figure.py'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command 'Get-Content SPEC.md; Get-Content sim.py; Get-Content bayes.py; Get-Content latent_filter.py; Get-Content e12_unlabelled_head.py; Get-Content e13_rungs_over_time.py; Get-Content figures/e13_figure.py'","aggregated_output":"# Observation-shift simulation study: specification (Claude, 17 Oct 2026)\r\n\r\n## Questions\r\n\r\nQ1 (justified cost). How much does the exact A-optimal predictor lose when applied unchanged under policy B? This is the loss no model trained only on A can avoid without giving up A accuracy.\r\nQ2 (excess reliance). Do end-to-end architectures, trained on raw values + masks under A, lose MORE than that justified cost on B (they rely on the observation pattern instead of the values, beyond what the information warrants), or less (they under-learn the mask)? Measured against the exact Bayes rules.\r\nQ3 (redundancy). How do Q1 and Q2 change as the values become more or less informative (noise sigma), i.e. as the mask's extra information over the values (Bayes mask gain) shrinks or grows?\r\nQ4 (sampling coverage). With UNINFORMATIVE observation in both hospitals, how much does each architecture lose beyond Bayes when only the density/regularity of sampling changes?\r\n\r\n## Data-generating process (module `sim.py`)\r\n\r\n- Time grid t = 0..T-1, T = 48. Latent state: stationary Gaussian AR(1), Z_0 ~ N(0,1), Z_t = rho Z_{t-1} + sqrt(1-rho^2) eps_t, rho = 0.95.\r\n- Outcome: Y = 1{Z_{T-1+h} > c}, horizon h = 6, threshold c = 1.0 (prevalence about 0.16). Z beyond T-1 continues the same AR(1).\r\n- Observation indicator O_t ~ Bernoulli(p_t). Value X_t = Z_t + sigma * eta_t (eta standard normal), available only when O_t = 1.\r\n- Policies (dataclass `Policy`, each with a name):\r\n  - `informative(gamma, rate)`: p_t = sigmoid(alpha + gamma Z_t), with alpha solved numerically so that E[p_t] = rate under Z ~ N(0,1).\r\n  - `uniform(rate)`: p_t = rate, independent of Z.\r\n  - `bursty(rate, stay)`: O_t a two-state Markov chain independent of Z, P(O_t = O_{t-1}) = stay, stationary marginal = rate (derive the switching probabilities; for rate 0.5 both stay-probabilities equal `stay`). O_0 drawn from the stationary marginal.\r\n- `simulate_latent(n, seed) -> dict(z: float32[n, T], z_future: float32[n], y: int8[n])`\r\n- `observe(latent, policy, sigma, seed) -> dict(x: float32[n, T] with NaN where unobserved, m: int8[n, T])`\r\n  The same latent cohort can be observed under several policies (paired test sets). Different seeds for latent and observation draws; all seeds explicit.\r\n\r\n## Exact Bayes predictors (module `bayes.py`, numpy only)\r\n\r\nGrid forward filter on z in [-5.5, 5.5] with G = 241 points (check that G = 481 changes Brier by < 1e-4). Transition kernel from the AR(1), rows renormalised. Per step likelihood given the ASSUMED observation model:\r\n- O_t = 1: p_assumed(O=1 | z) * Normal(x_t; z, sigma^2)\r\n- O_t = 0: 1 - p_assumed(O=1 | z)\r\nUniform/bursty policies are uninformative about z, so their likelihood factor reduces to Normal(x; z, sigma^2) when observed and 1 when not (\"values-only rule\"; it is exact Bayes for any Z-independent policy, including bursty).\r\nFinal prediction: P(Y=1 | history) = sum_z post_T(z) * [1 - Phi((c - rho^h z) / sqrt(1 - rho^(2h)))].\r\nFunction: `bayes_predict(x, m, sigma, assumed_policy) -> float64[n]`, vectorised over patients, work in log space or renormalise each step.\r\nRules used in analysis:\r\n- `A_rule`: assumed = training policy A. Applied to A test data = Bayes optimum under A. Applied to B data = transferred source rule.\r\n- `V_rule`: assumed uninformative (values-only). Exact Bayes under any Z-independent B; on A data it is the best rule that ignores what the pattern says about z.\r\nTests (pytest-free plain asserts in `test_sim_bayes.py`, runnable with numpy only):\r\n1. With an uninformative policy and every step observed, the grid filter matches an exact Kalman filter (posterior mean and variance at T-1, and P(Y=1)) to < 1e-3.\r\n2. With an uninformative policy and random missingness, same Kalman check (Kalman skips the update when unobserved).\r\n3. Calibration: on 20000 simulated patients under an informative policy, A_rule predictions binned in deciles match observed Y frequencies within Monte Carlo error; and A_rule Brier < V_rule Brier on A data.\r\n4. alpha solver gives the requested marginal rate (Monte Carlo check).\r\n5. Bursty chain has the requested marginal rate and stay probability.\r\n\r\n## Models (module `models.py`, PyTorch CPU)\r\n\r\nAll end-to-end on raw observed data. Output: one logit for Y at the end of the window. Hidden size 64 unless stated.\r\nPer-step features for recurrent models: x_locf (last observed value carried forward; 0 before the first observation), m_t, delta_t (steps since last observation, divided by T; GRU-D definition from Che et al. 2018).\r\n- `gru`: nn.GRU on [x_locf, m, delta], linear head on final hidden state.\r\n- `grud`: GRU-D (Che et al. 2018): input decay of x toward the training-set empirical mean of observed values, hidden-state decay from delta, mask as input. Implemented as an explicit loop over T with a GRUCell.\r\n- `cde`: Neural CDE (Kidger et al. 2020), own implementation, no torchcde. Control path channels: [t / T, x linearly interpolated between observations (forward-filled after the last and back-filled before the first observation; 0 if never observed), cumulative observation count / T]. Initial hidden h_0 = linear map of the path at t = 0. Vector field: MLP hidden -> 128 -> 128 -> hidden * channels with tanh output. Integrate with RK4-free Euler over the 47 path increments plus 2 substeps per increment (h += f(h) dX/substeps). Head on final hidden.\r\n- `transformer`: tokens only at observed steps: input projection of [x_t] plus sinusoidal embedding of t (d = 64), learned CLS token, 2 encoder layers, 4 heads, padding mask for absent steps. Patients with zero observations: CLS only. Head on CLS output.\r\nTraining: Adam lr 1e-3, weight decay 0, batch 256, BCE loss, max 120 epochs, early stopping on validation BCE (validation drawn under the TRAINING policy), patience 12, keep best checkpoint. Inputs computed once as tensors.\r\n\r\n## Experiments (runner `run_grid.py`)\r\n\r\nCohort sizes: train 8000, validation 2000, test 8000 latent patients; one test latent cohort per seed, observed under every test policy below with a common observation seed (paired across policies).\r\nSeeds: 0, 1, 2 (each seed redraws train, val, test and model init).\r\n\r\nE1 informative (Q1-Q3). Training policy A = informative(gamma, rate 0.3). Grid: sigma in {0.25, 0.5, 1.0, 2.0} x gamma in {1.0, 2.0}. Test policies for every fitted model: A itself, B_match = uniform(0.3), B_dense = uniform(0.6).\r\nE2 coverage (Q4). sigma = 0.5. Training policy A = uniform(0.5). Test policies: A, B_sparse = uniform(0.15), B_bursty = bursty(0.5, stay 0.9).\r\nFits: E1 8 configs x 4 archs x 3 seeds = 96; E2 1 x 4 x 3 = 12. Total 108.\r\nExecution: multiprocessing pool with `--workers` (default 4, the physical core count), torch.set_num_threads(1) in each worker, one fit per task, each fit writes its own shard directory `results/<exp>/<config>/<arch>/seed<k>/` with `preds.npz` (keys: one float32[n_test] array of predicted probabilities per test policy name, plus `y`), `meta.json` (config, arch, seed, best epoch, epochs run, train/val BCE history, wall time). Skip shards that already exist (resume). Also write `bayes.npz` once per config and seed with A_rule and V_rule predictions on every test policy (and `y`), computed in the main process before or after the fits.\r\nCLI: `python run_grid.py --exp E1|E2|all --workers 4 --out results [--smoke]`. `--smoke`: n_train 800, n_val 200, n_test 1000, 5 epochs, one config per experiment, all 4 archs, seed 0, into `results_smoke/`.\r\n\r\n## Analysis (`analyze.py`, numpy + pandas; `plot.py`, matplotlib)\r\n\r\nRisk R = Brier score (also report BCE). For each config, arch, seed, test policy B, with A the training policy:\r\n- Bayes mask gain on A: G = R_A(V_rule) - R_A(A_rule)  (redundancy measure; small G = near-redundant).\r\n- Justified transfer cost: J_B = R_B(A_rule) - R_B(V_rule)  (perfect A learning, applied to B, versus B's Bayes optimum).\r\n- Model source excess: S = R_A(model) - R_A(A_rule).\r\n- Model target excess over the transferred rule: X_B = R_B(model) - R_B(A_rule).\r\n- Model excess over B's optimum: R_B(model) - R_B(V_rule).\r\n- Reliance probe on A test data: regress logit(p_model) on [1, logit(p_V), logit(p_A) - logit(p_V)] by least squares, giving b_values and b_mask. Exact Bayes has b_values = 1, b_mask = 1 by construction. b_mask > 1 with b_values < 1 = substitution (pattern used instead of values). Also the same probe with R^2.\r\n- Mask gain captured: (R_A(V_rule) - R_A(model)) / G.\r\nAggregate: mean over seeds with seed-level min/max; paired differences between architectures with a patient-level bootstrap (1000 resamples, paired across arch and policy) for X_B and S.\r\nE2: excess over Bayes (= V_rule there) on A and on each B, per arch.\r\nOutputs: `analysis/e1_summary.csv`, `analysis/e2_summary.csv`, `analysis/contrasts.csv`, `analysis/summary.json` with the headline numbers. `plot.py`: (1) per sigma, X_B vs S per arch with the A_rule point at (0, 0) and J_B annotated; (2) b_mask and b_values vs sigma per arch; (3) E2 excess bars.\r\n\r\n## E3: exact redundancy, testing driven by recorded values only (Claude, 17 Oct 2026)\r\n\r\nQuestion: when the observation pattern is exactly redundant given the recorded values, so that the Bayes rule on A ignores it and the justified transfer cost is zero, do architectures still lose on B? Any loss on B is then pure shortcut use, decided by the architecture's prior off A's support.\r\n\r\nPolicy `recorded(gamma, rate, sigma)` in `sim.py` (name `recorded_g{gamma}_r{rate}_s{sigma}`, fields gamma, alpha, rate). Sequential over t: x_last = most recent recorded noisy value at s < t (0 if none yet); m_t ~ Bernoulli(sigmoid(alpha + gamma * x_last)); x_t = z_t + sigma * eps_t if m_t. Noise is drawn as a full (n, T) matrix before the masks. alpha is calibrated by bisection so that the mean of m over (patients, t) is `rate` on a fixed calibration cohort (n 4000, latent seed 990001, observation seed 990002, common random numbers so the bisection is monotone).\r\nWhy it is exactly redundant: P(m_t | z, past values) = P(m_t | past recorded values), so the policy factor cancels from the likelihood (missing at random). The exact Bayes rule on any such policy is the values-only rule. `bayes._is_informative` already treats non-`informative` policies as values-only, so A_rule = V_rule identically and G = J_B = 0.\r\nSetting: sigma 1.0, gamma 2.0, train A = recorded(2.0, 0.3, 1.0); test A, B_match = uniform(0.3), B_dense = uniform(0.6). Seed 0 only, standard cohort sizes and training. 4 fits; about 10 minutes on 4 workers.\r\nMetrics: S = R_A(model) - R_A(V_rule); X_B = R_B(model) - R_B(V_rule) (Brier, also BCE). Shortcut probe on each test policy: regress logit(p_model) - logit(p_V) on [1, n_obs / T, gap since last observation / T] (least squares, report coefficients and R^2); Bayes gives all zeros. Patient-level paired bootstrap (1000) for X_B per arch.\r\n\r\n## E4: can a network be taught to discount the testing pattern? (Claude, 18 Oct 2026)\r\n\r\nMotivation: blending the exact rules, logit p_w = logit p_V + w (logit p_A - logit p_V), traces a very lopsided trade-off. Near w = 1, error at A is flat (first-order optimum) while error at B falls linearly. So a small discount costs almost nothing at A and saves much at B. The fitted networks lie far above this curve. Question: can a standard shortcut-debiasing method bring a network onto it?\r\nMethod: product of experts (Clark et al. 2019; He et al. 2019). (1) Bias model b: GRU (hidden 64) on [0, m, delta] (the value channel zeroed), i.e. sees only who was tested and when, trained on A as usual. (2) Main model f: GRU with the standard features [x_locf, m, delta], trained on A with logit = f(x, m) + b(m), b frozen (its logits precomputed), same loss, optimiser, early stopping (validation loss also on f + b). (3) Deployment family: logit = f + w * b, w in {0, 0.1, ..., 1.0}. w = 1 is the model fitted to A; w = 0 removes the testing-pattern signal as far as the method can.\r\nSetting: E1 config sigma 1, gamma 2, seed 0 (same cohorts and seeds as E1 via run_grid helpers), test policies A, B_match, B_dense. Compare: the family's (Brier A, Brier B) points against the exact-rule blend curve and the plain E1 GRU (results/E1/sigma1_gamma2/gru/seed0).\r\nOutput: results/E4/sigma1_gamma2/seed0/{bias,poe_gru}/ with logits.npz (one float32[n_test] logit array per test policy, plus y) and meta.json.\r\n\r\n## E5: a learned model with a separate testing head, tempered at deployment (Claude, 18 Oct 2026)\r\n\r\nMotivation: E4 showed that a black-box network has no lever that turns down only the evidence carried by being tested. The exact rule has one, because the testing pattern enters the filter only through the factor P(m_t | Z_t). E5 asks whether a model that learns this structure from A's data, rather than being given it, gives a tempering curve that follows the exact blend curve.\r\nModel (`latent_filter.py`, PyTorch): a discrete latent-state filter with K = 32 states, all parameters learned by gradient descent on A's training data:\r\n- transition matrix: row-softmax of a learned K x K logit matrix (initialised near-diagonal: +4 on the diagonal, +2 on the neighbours);\r\n- initial distribution: softmax of learned logits;\r\n- value head: Gaussian emission, learned mean per state (initialised evenly from -2.5 to 2.5) and one shared learned log-sd;\r\n- testing head: P(m_t = 1 | state k) = sigmoid(c_k), learned;\r\n- outcome head: P(Y = 1 | state at T-1 = k) = sigmoid(u_k), learned; prediction = filtered belief at T-1 times sigmoid(u).\r\nForward filter in log space over t = 0..T-1: predict with the transition matrix; update with log N(x_t; mu_k, s) when m_t = 1 and with w * [m_t log sigmoid(c_k) + (1 - m_t) log(1 - sigmoid(c_k))] for every t. Training uses w = 1. Loss = BCE(y) + (1/T) * mean negative log marginal likelihood of (x, m), the latter from the same forward pass (log-sum-exp normalisers), so that the heads are proper likelihoods and not free features. Adam lr 1e-2, batch 512, up to 200 epochs, early stopping on validation loss (patience 15), torch.set_num_threads(4).\r\nDeployment family: the same trained model with w in {0, 0.05, ..., 1}.\r\nSetting: E1 config sigma 1, gamma 2, seed 0 (same cohorts as E1 via run_grid helpers); test policies A, B_match, B_dense.\r\nCompare: (Brier A, Brier B) of the tempered family against the exact blend curve and the plain E1 GRU. Also report the learned testing-head curve sigmoid(c_k) against the true policy sigmoid(alpha + gamma * mu_k) as a sanity check.\r\nExpectation, stated in advance: the simulator is itself a latent-state model of this kind, so success here is a proof of principle, not evidence for real data.\r\n\r\n## E6: the dial on real data from two hospitals (Claude, 19 Oct 2026)\r\n\r\nQuestion: on real data, does partial trust in the testing pattern (0 < w < 1) transfer better from hospital A to hospital B than full trust (w = 1) or ignoring it (w = 0, Futoma et al. 2021's \"fit with indicators, deploy without\")? And how many patients from B (a silent run-in) are needed to choose w, compared with recalibration and refitting?\r\nData: PhysioNet/CinC 2019 (docs/DATA.md). Hospital A = training_setA (Beth Israel, the hospital of MIMIC and of Futoma et al.); hospital B = training_setB (Emory).\r\nCohort: patients with at least 24 hourly rows (ICULOS 1..24 present); exclude patients with SepsisLabel = 1 in any of hours 1-24. Prediction time: end of hour 24. Outcome: SepsisLabel = 1 in any of hours 25-72 (patients leaving earlier count as 0).\r\nFeatures from hours 1-24, following Futoma: physiology = last observed value of each vital and lab (HR ... Platelets; not Unit1, Unit2) plus its square, Age, Gender, HospAdmTime; never-measured values filled with hospital A's median; all standardised with A's training statistics. Testing pattern = for each vital and lab: measured in hours 17-24 (0/1), measured at all in 1-24 (0/1), log(1 + number of measurements in 1-24).\r\nModel: L2-penalised logistic regression on all features, fit on 80% of A (rest = A test); penalty chosen by 5-fold cross-validation on A's training part. Also physiology-only and pattern-only models for reference.\r\nDial: logit_w = b0 + physiology terms + w * (pattern terms), w in 0, 0.1, ..., 1.5, applied to the A-fitted coefficients without refitting.\r\nEvaluation: AUROC, Brier and log loss on A test and on B (B test set below), with patient-level bootstrap intervals (1000) for the w curve on B.\r\nRun-in experiment: split B once into a run-in pool and a fixed B test set of 8,000 patients. For n_run in 100, 250, 500, 1000, 2500, 5000 (and the full pool), draw 50 random run-in samples; on each, choose: (a) w by log loss on the run-in, (b) intercept and slope recalibration of the w = 1 model, (c) w plus intercept, (d) a full refit on the run-in only (same penalty grid, CV on the run-in), (e) Futoma's w = 0 and the unadapted w = 1 as fixed references. Report mean and 10-90% range of B-test AUROC and log loss per method and n_run, plus the number of sepsis cases per run-in.\r\nDescriptive: per variable, the measurement rate in A and in B, and the AUROC of the pattern-only model within each hospital (how informative testing is in each).\r\n\r\n## E7: where does the best dial sit across many site pairs? (Claude, 19 Oct 2026)\r\n\r\nQuestion: across real source-target pairs, is the best trust weight w at an end (0 or 1) or in between, and does a dial tuned on target data beat choosing the better end on the same data? Open data only (no sign-in).\r\n\r\nSites:\r\n- **P19 (sepsis).** PhysioNet 2019, sites = hospital x unit: A-MICU, A-SICU, B-MICU, B-SICU (Unit1 = 1 medical, Unit2 = 1 surgical, taken at the first row with either set; patients with neither unit excluded). Cohort, features and outcome exactly as E6 (24-hour landmark; outcome sepsis in hours 25-72; physiology last values and squares plus Age, Gender, HospAdmTime; pattern: measured in hours 17-24, ever measured, log(1 + total count)). 12 directed pairs.\r\n- **P12 (mortality).** PhysioNet/CinC 2012 sets a, b, c (12,000 ICU stays, BIDMC, open, ODC-By; outcome In-hospital_death from Outcomes-*.txt), sites = ICUType 1 CCU, 2 cardiac surgery recovery, 3 medical, 4 surgical. Window: first 48 hours. Variables: all time-varying parameters except RecordID, Age, Gender, Height, ICUType, Weight (static); MechVent and Urine count as measurements. Physiology: last value of each variable in hours 0-48 and its square, plus Age, Gender, Height, Weight (static, -1 means missing). Pattern: measured in hours 40-48, ever measured in 0-48, log(1 + total count). 12 directed pairs.\r\n\r\nPer source site s: split 80/20 (seed fixed); medians, scaling and the L2 logistic penalty (5-fold CV on the E6 grid) from the 80%. Report held-out 20% AUROC and log loss. Dial as E6: logit_w = b0 + physiology + w * pattern, one fitted model, no refit.\r\n\r\nPer target t (all of t's patients, using s's medians and scaling):\r\n- w curve, w = 0 to 1.5 in steps of 0.05: log loss, Brier, AUROC.\r\n- Tuned comparison: 100 random 50/50 splits of t into tune and evaluation halves. On the tune half choose (i) w from the grid by log loss and (ii) the better end, w in {0, 1}, by log loss. Evaluate both on the evaluation half. Report the mean and 10-90% range of the difference (dial minus end) in log loss and AUROC, the share of splits where the chosen w is strictly inside (0.05 to 0.95), and the mean chosen w.\r\n- Also report n and outcome cases per site.\r\n\r\nOutputs: `analysis/e7_pairs.csv` (one row per dataset, source, target), `analysis/e7_curves.csv`, `analysis/e7_sites.csv`, `analysis/e7_summary.json`, figure script `figures/e7_figure.py` (small multiples: target log loss relative to w = 1 against w, one panel per pair, best w marked; Claude renders it).\r\n\r\n## E7b: dial above 1 versus recalibration (Claude, 19 Oct 2026)\r\n\r\nE7 found optima above 1 (up to the grid edge 1.5) with the largest log-loss gains, while AUROC fell, which suggests part of the gain is calibration (scaling the pattern term also stretches the predictions). E7b: same pairs and source fits as E7; extended grid w = 0 to 3; tuned comparison on 100 tune/evaluation splits between choosing the end (0 or 1), the dial on 0-1 and the dial on 0-3, each with and without intercept-and-slope recalibration fitted on the tune half (w chosen jointly with its recalibration). The dial counts as a real improvement over the binary choice only if `dial_recal` beats `end_recal`. Brief: tasks/e7b/e7b_recal.md.\r\n\r\n## E8: the dial with a realistic unit-level run-in (Claude and Wesley, 19 Oct 2026; pre-specified before running)\r\n\r\nDesign decision (Wesley, 19 Oct): the most realistic transfer is the same ICU type in another hospital (medical ICU at A to medical ICU at B), so these are the primary pairs. E7/E7b were exploratory and tuned on half of the target unit (about 12-65 cases), far more than a real run-in.\r\n\r\nPrimary pairs (PhysioNet 2019, E7 sites and source fits): A-MICU to B-MICU, B-MICU to A-MICU, A-SICU to B-SICU, B-SICU to A-SICU. Secondary: the other 8 P19 pairs.\r\n\r\nRun-in sizes, from a typical ICU (US median about 1,300 admissions a year, Checkley et al. 2014) and E6's eligibility (about half of stays): about 54 eligible patients per month. Sizes n_run = 54, 108, 162, 325, 650 (1, 2, 3, 6, 12 months), plus 1,300 (2 years) as a large reference. For each size, 200 random draws of a run-in from the target unit; evaluate on all other patients of the target unit.\r\n\r\nMethods, each chosen on the run-in only:\r\n- `keep`: fixed w = 1, no tuning (doing nothing);\r\n- `end`: w in {0, 1} by run-in log loss;\r\n- `dial`: w on 0 to 3, step 0.05, by run-in log loss;\r\n- `end_recal`, `dial_recal`: as above, each candidate followed by intercept-and-slope recalibration; because run-ins hold few cases, recalibration is penalised towards the identity (L2 penalty 1.0 on the intercept shift and on slope minus 1, added to the summed log loss); w chosen by the penalised run-in loss.\r\nTies in run-in loss (e.g. a run-in without cases) are broken towards w = 1.\r\n\r\nPrimary outcome: AUROC on the evaluation patients, `dial_recal` minus `end_recal` and `dial_recal` minus `keep`, at the 3-month run-in (n_run = 162), per primary pair: mean, 10-90% range, share of draws with a gain. Secondary: log loss; all sizes; the other methods; secondary pairs; run-in case counts.\r\n\r\n## E9: E8 with corrected tuning (Claude, 20 Oct 2026; exploratory, designed after seeing E8)\r\n\r\nE8 chose w by run-in log loss. At the target unit the source model is overconfident, so log loss favours lowering w (shrinking predictions), while the best ranking lies at w of about 2-3 (E7b curves); with 3-6 cases the choice is also mostly noise. E9 repeats E8 (same pairs, sources, run-in sizes, 200 draws, evaluation on the rest of the target unit, new fixed seed) with tuning that separates calibration from the dial, targets ranking, and defaults to the trained model. Because the methods were designed after seeing E8 on the same pairs, E9 is exploratory; confirmation needs simulation or unseen data.\r\n\r\nMethods (grid w = 0 to 3, step 0.05; run-in only):\r\n- `keep`: w = 1 (reference; recalibration does not change AUROC).\r\n- `dial_recal`: as E8 (reference).\r\n- `dial_recal_shrink`: minimise, over w, the penalised recalibrated run-in loss of E8 plus tau * (w - 1)^2 with tau = 2 (in summed log-loss units, i.e. a normal prior on w centred at 1 with sd 0.5), so w moves away from 1 only as the run-in evidence grows.\r\n- `dial_auc`: w with the highest run-in AUROC; ties, and run-ins with no case or no non-case, go to w = 1.\r\n- `dial_auc_shrink`: w with the highest run-in AUROC minus 0.02 * (w - 1)^2 (prefers w = 1 unless the run-in AUROC gain is clear); same tie rule.\r\n\r\nPrimary outcome as E8: AUROC difference to `keep` on the 4 primary pairs at the 3-month run-in (mean, 10-90%, share of draws with a gain), for each method; secondary: all sizes, log loss (with each method's recalibration applied; `dial_auc` variants use the E8 penalised recalibration at their chosen w), secondary pairs.\r\n\r\n## E10: which part shifted? A direct diagnostic per pair (Claude, 20 Oct 2026)\r\n\r\nQuestion: between two sites, does the meaning of the testing pattern change (observation shift in the sense the dial targets), does the physiology part change, or neither? E7-E9 inferred this indirectly from where the best w lay.\r\n\r\nSites: P19 unit sites (A-MICU, A-SICU, B-MICU, B-SICU) and the two whole hospitals (A-all, B-all, E6 cohort); P12 ICU types (CCU, CSRU, MICU, SICU). Pairs: all ordered pairs within P19 units (12), A-all to B-all and back (2), all ordered P12 pairs (12).\r\n\r\nFor each site, one fixed 50/50 split into a fit half and an evaluation half (seed stated). For each pair s to t and each feature set F in {pattern only, physiology only, combined}:\r\n- `transferred`: L2 logistic (5-fold CV over the E6 grid) fitted on s's fit half with s's medians and scaling, evaluated on t's evaluation half;\r\n- `local`: the same model class fitted on t's fit half with t's own medians and scaling, evaluated on t's evaluation half.\r\n- Transfer gap = AUROC(local) minus AUROC(transferred), and the same for log loss (transferred minus local), with a paired bootstrap 95% interval over t's evaluation patients (500 resamples).\r\nA large pattern gap with a small physiology gap means the testing pattern's meaning shifted; the reverse means physiology shifted. Also report each site's own `local` AUROC per feature set (how informative each part is at that site).\r\n\r\nCoverage and rates per site: share of each physiology variable that is imputed (never measured in the window), and measurement rates; per pair, the mean absolute difference in measurement rates across variables and in imputed shares.\r\n\r\n## E11: realistic run-in with safe tuning on the 2012 mortality pairs (Claude and Wesley, 20 Oct 2026; pre-specified before running)\r\n\r\nMotivation: E10 found that the testing pattern's meaning shifts mainly in the PhysioNet 2012 mortality pairs, and death is far more frequent than sepsis (about 14%), so a run-in holds many more cases. E11 applies E9's corrected tuning there.\r\n\r\nPairs: all 12 ordered P12 pairs (E7 source fits, same preprocessing). Primary: all 12 pairs pooled (mean over pairs). Key subgroup, named in advance from E10: the four pairs whose pattern gap was clearly above zero (CCU to SICU, CSRU to CCU, CSRU to SICU, SICU to CSRU). Caveat: E10 used the same target patients, so the subgroup is not independent.\r\n\r\nRun-in sizes as E8: n_run = 54, 108, 162, 325, 650 (1, 2, 3, 6, 12 months of a typical ICU), plus 1,300 where the unit allows; 200 draws per size; evaluation on all other patients of the target unit; skip a size if fewer than 500 evaluation patients would remain.\r\n\r\nMethods (as E9, grid w = 0 to 3 step 0.05, run-in only): `keep` (w = 1), `end_recal` (w in {0, 1} with E8's penalised recalibration), `dial_recal` (E8), `dial_recal_shrink` (tau = 2), `dial_auc_shrink` (penalty 0.02). Ties and run-ins without a case or a non-case go to w = 1.\r\n\r\nPrimary outcome: AUROC difference to `keep` at the 3-month run-in (n_run = 162), for `dial_recal_shrink`: mean over the 12 pairs, and per pair mean, 10-90% range and share of draws with a gain. Secondary: the other methods (including `dial_recal_shrink` minus `end_recal`), all sizes, log loss, the subgroup.\r\n\r\n## E12: re-learning the testing head at B without outcomes (Claude and Wesley, 23 Oct 2026; exploratory, quick version)\r\n\r\nQuestion (from a teammate, 22 Oct): rung 3 re-learns only the testing head. The testing head is a likelihood of test orders given the latent state, so it can be fitted on B's records without outcome labels, which exist for every patient. How does that compare with the labelled rungs, as a function of how much data each uses?\r\n\r\nModel: the E5 filter already trained at A (`results/E5/sigma1_gamma2/seed0/filter/params.npz`, sigma 1, A = informative gamma 2, rate 0.3). No retraining at A.\r\n\r\nTarget policies at B (sigma 1, rate 0.3 unless stated): `B_less` informative gamma 1 (less selective); `B_more` informative gamma 3.5 (more selective; the dial in [0, 1] cannot help here, see docs/GAP.md \"The dial above 1\"); `B_match` uniform 0.3; `B_dense` uniform 0.6.\r\n\r\nData at B: test cohort n = 8,000 (E5 test latent seed, new observation seed per policy); labelled run-in pools and unlabelled pools from new latent seeds, disjoint from the test cohort. Outcome prevalence is about 16%.\r\n\r\nMethods (all start from the A parameters; only the testing-head logits c, 32 numbers, or w change):\r\n- `keep`: w = 1. `drop`: w = 0.\r\n- `dial_L` (rung 1): w on the grid 0, 0.05, ..., 1 minimising log loss on the labelled run-in (ties to 1).\r\n- `head_L` (rung 3, labelled): refit c on the labelled run-in with the E5 training loss (BCE + per-step NLL of values and masks), all other parameters frozen.\r\n- `head_U` (rung 3, no outcomes): refit c on an unlabelled pool by the per-step NLL of values and masks only, all other parameters frozen.\r\n- `oracle`: exact Bayes predictor under the B policy (`bayes.bayes_predict`).\r\nRefits: Adam lr 1e-2, minibatch 256, 80 steps, initialised at A's c; no early stopping.\r\n\r\nSizes: labelled run-in n_L = 30, 125, 500 patients (about 5, 20, 80 outcome cases); unlabelled pool n_U = 200, 2,000 patients. 3 draws per size and policy. Quick version; more draws only if the answer is unclear.\r\n\r\nOutputs: per policy, method, size and draw: Brier, log loss, AUROC on the B test cohort, and regret = Brier minus oracle Brier; the refitted c curves for `head_U` at n_U = 2,000 against the true B testing curve on the latent grid (sanity check; states need not align, see E5). Files `analysis/e12_runs.csv`, `analysis/e12_summary.csv`, `analysis/e12_c_curves.csv`.\r\n\r\nExpectation, stated in advance: in this simulator testing depends only on the latent state, so B's change of policy is the case `head_U` is built for; it should approach the oracle without labels, and handle `B_more`, where the dial cannot. A shift in what a test means for the outcome beyond the state cannot be simulated here, and `head_U` would not detect it.\r\n\r\n## E13: which rung when? Performance over weeks of deployment (Claude and Wesley, 23 Oct 2026; simulation)\r\n\r\nQuestion: as data from hospital B accrue week by week after deployment, which rung of the ladder is best at each time, and where do the curves cross? Main figure: x = weeks since deployment (log scale), y = regret against the exact optimum at B, one curve per rung, the best rung per week shaded.\r\n\r\nLadder (ordered by outcome labels needed; Wesley, 23 Oct; docs/ARGUMENT.md section 10):\r\n- R0 `keep`: A's model.\r\n- R1 `head_U`: re-learn the testing-head logits c on all of B's records so far, without outcomes (value-and-mask likelihood only), other parameters frozen.\r\n- R2 `head_U_dial`: R1, then one weight w in [0, 1] on the re-learned testing evidence, chosen on the labelled patients so far by penalised log loss: sum of log losses + 2 * (w - 1)^2 (E9's prior, sd 0.5 around 1); no outcome case yet -> w = 1.\r\n- R4 `finetune`: all parameters from A's values, on the labelled patients so far, loss BCE + per-step NLL (E5 loss); no outcome case yet -> A's model.\r\n- Baseline `dial_only`: the original dial on A's model (no re-learning), same penalised rule as R2.\r\n- R3 (dial per test group) needs several tests; the simulator has one. Left for the multi-test simulator.\r\n- `oracle`: exact Bayes predictor under B's policy.\r\n\r\nRealistic rates: outcome threshold raised from 1.0 to 2.05 so that about 2% of patients have the outcome (as sepsis in E6). This changes the A model: retrain the E5 filter at A (informative gamma 2, rate 0.3, sigma 1) with this outcome, n_train 16,000, n_val 4,000 (about 320 and 80 cases), otherwise as E5 (`latent_filter.train_filter`). Implementation: set `sim.THRESHOLD` and `bayes.THRESHOLD` (bayes imports it by value) to 2.05 at import time of the E13 script, so spawned workers see it too.\r\n\r\nTime axis: weeks t = 1, 2, 4, 8, 13, 26, 52, 104; 25 admissions per week (1,300 a year, a median US ICU), so n(t) = 25 t patients (about 0.5 outcome cases a week). All n(t) patients' testing records are available for R1; their outcomes are available for R2, R4 and `dial_only` (outcome delay ignored: the 6-hour horizon is short against a week).\r\n\r\nB policies: as E12 (`B_less`, `B_more`, `B_match`, `B_dense`). Test cohort 10,000 patients (about 200 cases), fixed. 5 draws of the deployment stream per week and policy; the stream is nested (week t's patients are the first 25 t of one stream of 2,600 per draw), so curves over weeks are paired.\r\n\r\nRefits (R1 and R4): Adam, minibatch 256 with replacement, settings from E12b if it converges (lr 0.05, 160 steps), else stated with the result.\r\n\r\nOutputs: `analysis/e13_runs.csv` (policy, method, week, n patients, n cases, draw, Brier, log loss, AUROC, regret, w), `analysis/e13_summary.csv`, `figures/e13_rungs_over_time.png` (one panel per policy, mean regret with min-max band over draws, shading of the best rung per week).\r\n\r\nExpectation, stated in advance: R1 is best from the first week when B's testing still depends on the state; R2 adds little here, because the simulator shifts only testing (its job is a shift in a test's meaning for the outcome, which cannot be simulated with this simulator); R4 overtakes only after many months, if at all within two years. A rule that chooses the rung from run-in data alone is the next step, not part of E13.\r\n\r\n## E14: a shift only outcomes can reveal (Claude and Wesley, 25 Oct 2026; simulation, quick version)\r\n\r\nQuestion: E12-E13 shifted only how testing depends on the patient's state, which B's unlabelled records reveal, and head_U won at every run-in length. Is there a shift that head_U cannot fix, where the outcome-based rungs earn their place, and does head_U at least do no harm there? This turns \"which rung\" into \"which kind of shift\", and E14 checks that reading.\r\n\r\nSimulator extension (new module `sim_u.py`, `sim.py` and `bayes.py` unchanged): each patient has a hidden binary trait u ~ Bernoulli(0.3), independent of the AR(1) path z (think: a risk factor the doctor sees at the bedside but the model does not, e.g. frailty or a worrying history). The outcome uses it at both hospitals: y = 1[z_future + 1.0 * u > threshold], threshold chosen so that about 2% of patients have the outcome (solve by quadrature, assert 1-3% on 20,000 patients). Testing: P(test at t) = sigmoid(alpha + gamma * z_t + beta * u), alpha set so the marginal testing rate is 0.3 (z ~ N(0, 1) stationary, u mixture); values as before, sigma 1. Policy \"u-informative\" with parameters (gamma, beta).\r\n\r\nTwo directions:\r\n- `gain`: A tests on the state only (gamma 2, beta 0); B's doctors also test on the trait (gamma 2, beta 2). At B a test means more for the outcome than at A. B's records alone show that testing varies between patients more than the state explains, but not that this extra testing predicts the outcome. Expected: head_U cannot exploit it (A's states do not represent u, and the outcome head is frozen); rungs with outcomes can, given enough cases.\r\n- `lose`: A tests on both (gamma 2, beta 2); B on the state only (gamma 2, beta 0). At B a test means less. Expected: the A model learns states that carry u (persistent, from the testing pattern); head_U re-learns that testing at B no longer differs between these states and so stops reading u into tests, without outcomes.\r\n\r\nA models: one per direction, as E13 (E5 filter, K = 32, n_train 16,000, n_val 4,000, `latent_filter.train_filter`), trained on A's policy with the new outcome; cached under `results/E14/a_model_<direction>/`.\r\n\r\nOracle: exact Bayes predictor under B's policy, a mixture over u: for each u run the 241-point grid filter with alpha + beta * u, keeping the log evidence of the history; P(y | h) = sum over u of P(u | h) * P(z_future > threshold - u | h, u). Checks: with beta = 0, P(u = 1 | h) is 0.3 for every patient; with beta = 0 and the outcome shift set to 0, the predictor equals `bayes.bayes_predict` at the same threshold to 1e-10.\r\n\r\nMethods per (direction, draw, week), as E13 plus a labelled testing-head refit:\r\n- `keep`, `head_U` (E12's `_refit_testing_head` without labels), `dial_only` (penalised w in [0, 1], E13 rule), `head_L` (E12's `_refit_testing_head` with the stream's outcomes: BCE + NLL, c only), `finetune_lr05` and `finetune_lr005` (E13's `_finetune` at lr 0.05 and 0.005), `oracle`. Refits: 160 steps, minibatch 256 with replacement, lr 0.05 unless named. Zero outcome cases: labelled methods return A's model.\r\n\r\nQuick design: weeks 4, 13, 52, 104 at 25 patients a week (nested streams of 2,600, as E13), 2 draws, test cohort 10,000. Outputs `analysis/e14_runs.csv`, `analysis/e14_summary.csv`, `figures/e14_meaning_shift.png` (one panel per direction, same style as the fixed E13 figure: log axes, no shading). Also report per direction the regret of `keep` and the oracle's AUROC gain over `keep`, so we know how large the shift is.\r\n\r\nExpectation, stated in advance: `lose`: head_U recovers most of keep's regret from week 4. `gain`: head_U close to keep (neither helps nor harms); head_L or fine-tuning gain only with many cases, if at all within 2 years. If `gain` shows no method gaining within 2 years, the reading is that a shift in what tests mean for the outcome cannot be fixed from one ICU's run-in, only detected.\r\n\"\"\"Simulation utilities for the observation-shift study (NumPy only).\"\"\"\r\n\r\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\r\nfrom functools import lru_cache\r\nimport math\r\n\r\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\n\r\nT = 48\r\nRHO = 0.95\r\nHORIZON = 6\r\nTHRESHOLD = 1.0\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _sigmoid(x):\r\n    \"\"\"Numerically stable logistic function that accepts NumPy arrays.\"\"\"\r\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    out = np.empty_like(x)\r\n    positive = x >= 0\r\n    out[positive] = 1.0 / (1.0 + np.exp(-x[positive]))\r\n    ex = np.exp(x[~positive])\r\n    out[~positive] = ex / (1.0 + ex)\r\n    return out\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _alpha_for_rate(gamma, rate):\r\n    \"\"\"Solve E[sigmoid(alpha + gamma Z)] = rate by Gaussian quadrature.\"\"\"\r\n    if not 0.0 < rate < 1.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"rate must lie strictly between 0 and 1\")\r\n    if gamma < 0.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"gamma must be nonnegative\")\r\n    if gamma == 0.0:\r\n        return math.log(rate / (1.0 - rate))\r\n\r\n    # Hermite nodes integrate against exp(-x**2); sqrt(2) transforms them\r\n    # to a standard normal.  The fixed quadrature makes policy construction\r\n    # deterministic and avoids a costly Monte Carlo root solve.\r\n    nodes, weights = np.polynomial.hermite.hermgauss(80)\r\n    z = math.sqrt(2.0) * nodes\r\n    weights = weights / math.sqrt(math.pi)\r\n\r\n    lo, hi = -30.0 - gamma, 30.0 + gamma\r\n    for _ in range(80):\r\n        mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi)\r\n        marginal = float(np.dot(weights, _sigmoid(mid + gamma * z)))\r\n        if marginal < rate:\r\n            lo = mid\r\n        else:\r\n            hi = mid\r\n    return 0.5 * (lo + hi)\r\n\r\n\r\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\r\nclass Policy:\r\n    \"\"\"Observation policy and its parameters.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n    name: str\r\n    rate: float\r\n    gamma: float = 0.0\r\n    alpha: float = 0.0\r\n    stay: float = 0.0\r\n\r\n\r\ndef informative(gamma, rate):\r\n    \"\"\"Return an informative policy with marginal observation rate ``rate``.\"\"\"\r\n    gamma = float(gamma)\r\n    rate = float(rate)\r\n    alpha = _alpha_for_rate(gamma, rate)\r\n    return Policy(name=f\"informative_g{gamma:g}_r{rate:g}\", rate=rate,\r\n                  gamma=gamma, alpha=alpha)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _recorded_mask(z, noisy, uniforms, alpha, gamma):\r\n    \"\"\"Draw recorded-value-driven masks from fixed noise and uniforms.\"\"\"\r\n    n = z.shape[0]\r\n    mask = np.empty((n, T), dtype=bool)\r\n    x_last = np.zeros(n, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    for t in range(T):\r\n        p = _sigmoid(alpha + gamma * x_last)\r\n        mask[:, t] = uniforms[:, t] < p\r\n        x_last = np.where(mask[:, t], noisy[:, t], x_last)\r\n    return mask\r\n\r\n\r\n@lru_cache(maxsize=32)\r\ndef _alpha_for_recorded_rate(gamma, rate, sigma):\r\n    \"\"\"Calibrate recorded-policy alpha on the fixed E3 cohort and draws.\"\"\"\r\n    latent = simulate_latent(4000, seed=990001)\r\n    z = np.asarray(latent[\"z\"], dtype=np.float64)\r\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(990002)\r\n    # Common random numbers make the calibration deterministic across trials.\r\n    noisy = z + sigma * rng.standard_normal((z.shape[0], T), dtype=np.float64)\r\n    uniforms = rng.random((z.shape[0], T))\r\n\r\n    lo, hi = -30.0 - gamma, 30.0 + gamma\r\n    best_alpha = 0.5 * (lo + hi)\r\n    best_error = float(\"inf\")\r\n    for _ in range(80):\r\n        mid = 0.5 * (lo + hi)\r\n        mask = _recorded_mask(z, noisy, uniforms, mid, gamma)\r\n        empirical_rate = float(mask.mean())\r\n        error = abs(empirical_rate - rate)\r\n        if error < best_error:\r\n            best_error = error\r\n            best_alpha = mid\r\n        if empirical_rate < rate:\r\n            lo = mid\r\n        else:\r\n            hi = mid\r\n    return float(best_alpha)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef recorded(gamma, rate, sigma):\r\n    \"\"\"Return a policy whose mask depends on the last recorded noisy value.\"\"\"\r\n    gamma = float(gamma)\r\n    rate = float(rate)\r\n    sigma = float(sigma)\r\n    if gamma < 0.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"gamma must be nonnegative\")\r\n    if not 0.0 < rate < 1.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"rate must lie strictly between 0 and 1\")\r\n    if sigma < 0.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"sigma must be nonnegative\")\r\n    alpha = _alpha_for_recorded_rate(gamma, rate, sigma)\r\n    return Policy(name=f\"recorded_g{gamma:g}_r{rate:g}_s{sigma:g}\",\r\n                  rate=rate, gamma=gamma, alpha=alpha)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef recorded_probs(x, m, policy):\r\n    \"\"\"Return sequential recorded-policy probabilities for observed histories.\"\"\"\r\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    m = np.asarray(m, dtype=bool)\r\n    if x.ndim != 2 or x.shape != m.shape or x.shape[1] != T:\r\n        raise ValueError(f\"x and m must have matching shape (n, {T})\")\r\n    if not getattr(policy, \"name\", \"\").startswith(\"recorded\"):\r\n        raise ValueError(\"policy must be a recorded policy\")\r\n    probabilities = np.empty(x.shape, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    x_last = np.zeros(x.shape[0], dtype=np.float64)\r\n    for t in range(T):\r\n        probabilities[:, t] = _sigmoid(policy.alpha + policy.gamma * x_last)\r\n        x_last = np.where(m[:, t], x[:, t], x_last)\r\n    return probabilities\r\n\r\n\r\ndef uniform(rate):\r\n    \"\"\"Return a Z-independent Bernoulli observation policy.\"\"\"\r\n    rate = float(rate)\r\n    if not 0.0 <= rate <= 1.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"rate must lie between 0 and 1\")\r\n    return Policy(name=f\"uniform_{rate:g}\", rate=rate)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef bursty(rate, stay):\r\n    \"\"\"Return a stationary two-state Markov observation policy.\r\n\r\n    ``stay`` is the stationary, aggregate probability of repeating the prior\r\n    state.  The two conditional switching probabilities are derived from the\r\n    stationary flow constraint.  This gives equal conditional stay\r\n    probabilities when ``rate == 0.5``.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    rate = float(rate)\r\n    stay = float(stay)\r\n    if not 0.0 < rate < 1.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"rate must lie strictly between 0 and 1\")\r\n    if not 0.0 <= stay <= 1.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"stay must lie between 0 and 1\")\r\n    p10 = (1.0 - stay) / (2.0 * rate)       # 1 -> 0\r\n    p01 = (1.0 - stay) / (2.0 * (1.0 - rate))  # 0 -> 1\r\n    if p10 > 1.0 + 1e-12 or p01 > 1.0 + 1e-12:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"requested rate/stay pair has no valid two-state chain\")\r\n    return Policy(name=f\"bursty_{rate:g}_s{stay:g}\", rate=rate, stay=stay)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef simulate_latent(n, seed):\r\n    \"\"\"Simulate stationary Gaussian AR(1) paths and the horizon outcome.\"\"\"\r\n    n = int(n)\r\n    if n < 0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be nonnegative\")\r\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\r\n    total_steps = T + HORIZON\r\n    eps = rng.standard_normal((n, total_steps), dtype=np.float64)\r\n    z = np.empty_like(eps)\r\n    z[:, 0] = eps[:, 0]\r\n    innovation_sd = math.sqrt(1.0 - RHO * RHO)\r\n    for t in range(1, total_steps):\r\n        z[:, t] = RHO * z[:, t - 1] + innovation_sd * eps[:, t]\r\n    z_window = z[:, :T].astype(np.float32)\r\n    z_future = z[:, T + HORIZON - 1].astype(np.float32)\r\n    y = (z_future > THRESHOLD).astype(np.int8)\r\n    return {\"z\": z_window, \"z_future\": z_future, \"y\": y}\r\n\r\n\r\ndef observe(latent, policy, sigma, seed):\r\n    \"\"\"Draw masks and noisy values for an existing latent cohort.\"\"\"\r\n    z = np.asarray(latent[\"z\"], dtype=np.float64)\r\n    if z.ndim != 2 or z.shape[1] != T:\r\n        raise ValueError(f\"latent['z'] must have shape (n, {T})\")\r\n    sigma = float(sigma)\r\n    if sigma < 0.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"sigma must be nonnegative\")\r\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(seed)\r\n    n = z.shape[0]\r\n\r\n    if policy.name.startswith(\"recorded\"):\r\n        # E3 draws the complete noise matrix before generating any masks.\r\n        noisy = z + sigma * rng.standard_normal((n, T), dtype=np.float64)\r\n        uniforms = rng.random((n, T))\r\n        mask = _recorded_mask(z, noisy, uniforms, policy.alpha, policy.gamma)\r\n    elif policy.name.startswith(\"informative\"):\r\n        p = _sigmoid(policy.alpha + policy.gamma * z)\r\n        mask = (rng.random((n, T)) < p)\r\n    elif policy.name.startswith(\"bursty\"):\r\n        # P(1->0) and P(0->1) preserve the requested stationary rate.\r\n        p10 = (1.0 - policy.stay) / (2.0 * policy.rate)\r\n        p01 = (1.0 - policy.stay) / (2.0 * (1.0 - policy.rate))\r\n        mask = np.empty((n, T), dtype=bool)\r\n        mask[:, 0] = rng.random(n) < policy.rate\r\n        for t in range(1, T):\r\n            u = rng.random(n)\r\n            previous = mask[:, t - 1]\r\n            mask[:, t] = np.where(previous, u >= p10, u < p01)\r\n    else:\r\n        mask = rng.random((n, T)) < policy.rate\r\n\r\n    if not policy.name.startswith(\"recorded\"):\r\n        noisy = z + sigma * rng.standard_normal((n, T), dtype=np.float64)\r\n    x = np.where(mask, noisy, np.nan).astype(np.float32)\r\n    return {\"x\": x, \"m\": mask.astype(np.int8)}\r\n\r\n\"\"\"Grid-based exact Bayes predictors for the observation-shift study.\"\"\"\r\n\r\nfrom functools import lru_cache\r\nimport math\r\n\r\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\nfrom sim import HORIZON, RHO, THRESHOLD, T\r\n\r\n\r\nGRID_MIN = -5.5\r\nGRID_MAX = 5.5\r\nDEFAULT_G = 241\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _erf_array(values):\r\n    \"\"\"Apply the platform's accurate math.erf to an ndarray.\"\"\"\r\n    values = np.asarray(values, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    flat = values.ravel()\r\n    return np.fromiter((math.erf(float(v)) for v in flat), dtype=np.float64,\r\n                       count=flat.size).reshape(values.shape)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _normal_cdf(values):\r\n    return 0.5 * (1.0 + _erf_array(np.asarray(values, dtype=np.float64) / math.sqrt(2.0)))\r\n\r\n\r\n@lru_cache(maxsize=4)\r\ndef _grid_and_transition(G):\r\n    \"\"\"Return grid, discretized stationary prior, and row-normalized kernel.\"\"\"\r\n    G = int(G)\r\n    if G < 3:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"G must be at least 3\")\r\n    grid = np.linspace(GRID_MIN, GRID_MAX, G, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    # Evaluate the stationary density and conditional transition density at\r\n    # grid points, then normalize.  The common density constants and grid\r\n    # spacing cancel in these discrete distributions.\r\n    prior = np.exp(-0.5 * grid * grid)\r\n    prior /= prior.sum()\r\n\r\n    sd = math.sqrt(1.0 - RHO * RHO)\r\n    residual = (grid[None, :] - RHO * grid[:, None]) / sd\r\n    transition = np.exp(-0.5 * residual * residual)\r\n    transition /= transition.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\r\n    grid.setflags(write=False)\r\n    prior.setflags(write=False)\r\n    transition.setflags(write=False)\r\n    return grid, prior, transition\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _is_informative(policy):\r\n    return getattr(policy, \"name\", \"\").startswith(\"informative\")\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _bayes_predict_grid(x, m, sigma, assumed_policy, G):\r\n    \"\"\"Grid-size implementation used by the public predictor and convergence test.\r\n\r\n    ``assumed_policy`` controls whether the observation pattern contributes\r\n    information about the latent state.  Any Z-independent policy (uniform or\r\n    bursty) is treated as values-only.  ``G`` is an optional grid-size\r\n    override used for the prescribed 241-versus-481 convergence check.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    m = np.asarray(m, dtype=bool)\r\n    if x.ndim != 2 or x.shape != m.shape or x.shape[1] != T:\r\n        raise ValueError(f\"x and m must have matching shape (n, {T})\")\r\n    sigma = float(sigma)\r\n    if sigma <= 0.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"sigma must be positive\")\r\n\r\n    grid, initial, transition = _grid_and_transition(int(G))\r\n    n = x.shape[0]\r\n    posterior = np.broadcast_to(initial, (n, initial.size)).copy()\r\n    informative = _is_informative(assumed_policy)\r\n    tiny = np.finfo(np.float64).tiny\r\n    log_normal_scale = -math.log(sigma)\r\n\r\n    if informative:\r\n        gamma = float(assumed_policy.gamma)\r\n        alpha = float(assumed_policy.alpha)\r\n        log_p = -np.logaddexp(0.0, -(alpha + gamma * grid))\r\n        log_not_p = -np.logaddexp(0.0, alpha + gamma * grid)\r\n\r\n    for t in range(T):\r\n        # Rows of the kernel are previous states; this matrix product\r\n        # propagates every patient's posterior in one BLAS operation.\r\n        predicted = posterior @ transition\r\n        observed = m[:, t]\r\n        if informative:\r\n            log_likelihood = np.broadcast_to(log_not_p, predicted.shape).copy()\r\n            if np.any(observed):\r\n                residual = (x[observed, t, None] - grid[None, :]) / sigma\r\n                log_likelihood[observed] = (\r\n                    log_p[None, :] + log_normal_scale - 0.5 * residual * residual\r\n                )\r\n        else:\r\n            log_likelihood = np.zeros_like(predicted)\r\n            if np.any(observed):\r\n                residual = (x[observed, t, None] - grid[None, :]) / sigma\r\n                log_likelihood[observed] = (\r\n                    log_normal_scale - 0.5 * residual * residual\r\n                )\r\n\r\n        log_weights = np.log(np.maximum(predicted, tiny)) + log_likelihood\r\n        log_weights -= np.max(log_weights, axis=1, keepdims=True)\r\n        posterior = np.exp(log_weights)\r\n        posterior /= posterior.sum(axis=1, keepdims=True)\r\n\r\n    future_sd = math.sqrt(1.0 - RHO ** (2 * HORIZON))\r\n    future_mean = (RHO ** HORIZON) * grid\r\n    event_given_state = 1.0 - _normal_cdf((THRESHOLD - future_mean) / future_sd)\r\n    return np.asarray(posterior @ event_given_state, dtype=np.float64)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef bayes_predict(x, m, sigma, assumed_policy):\r\n    \"\"\"Return ``P(Y=1 | x, m)`` using the specified 241-point grid filter.\"\"\"\r\n    return _bayes_predict_grid(x, m, sigma, assumed_policy, DEFAULT_G)\r\n\r\n\"\"\"Learned discrete latent-state filter for the E5 observation-shift experiment.\r\n\r\nThe NumPy implementation is intentionally kept in lockstep with the Torch\r\nforward pass so the recursion can be inspected and checked without Torch.\r\n\"\"\"\r\n\r\nfrom __future__ import annotations\r\n\r\nimport argparse\r\nimport copy\r\nimport json\r\nimport math\r\nimport os\r\nfrom pathlib import Path\r\nimport tempfile\r\nimport time\r\nfrom typing import Dict, Iterable, Mapping, Tuple\r\n\r\nimport numpy as np\r\n\r\nimport bayes\r\nimport run_grid\r\nimport sim\r\n\r\ntry:\r\n    import torch\r\n    from torch import nn\r\n    from torch.nn import functional as F\r\nexcept ImportError:  # NumPy reference remains usable in the supplied runtime.\r\n    torch = None\r\n    nn = None\r\n    F = None\r\n\r\n\r\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\r\nDEFAULT_WEIGHTS = np.round(np.arange(0.0, 1.0001, 0.05), 2)\r\nPARAMETER_NAMES = (\"transition_logits\", \"initial_logits\", \"mu\", \"log_sd\", \"c\", \"u\")\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _logsumexp(values: np.ndarray, axis: int, keepdims: bool = False) -> np.ndarray:\r\n    values = np.asarray(values, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    maximum = np.max(values, axis=axis, keepdims=True)\r\n    result = maximum + np.log(np.sum(np.exp(values - maximum), axis=axis, keepdims=True))\r\n    if not keepdims:\r\n        result = np.squeeze(result, axis=axis)\r\n    return result\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _log_softmax(values: np.ndarray, axis: int = -1) -> np.ndarray:\r\n    values = np.asarray(values, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    return values - _logsumexp(values, axis=axis, keepdims=True)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _sigmoid(values: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n    values = np.asarray(values, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    output = np.empty_like(values)\r\n    positive = values >= 0.0\r\n    output[positive] = 1.0 / (1.0 + np.exp(-values[positive]))\r\n    exponent = np.exp(values[~positive])\r\n    output[~positive] = exponent / (1.0 + exponent)\r\n    return output\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _check_inputs(x: np.ndarray, m: np.ndarray, parameters: Mapping[str, np.ndarray], w: float):\r\n    x = np.asarray(x, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    m = np.asarray(m)\r\n    if x.ndim != 2 or m.shape != x.shape:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"x and m must have matching shape [n, T]\")\r\n    if x.shape[1] < 1:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"time dimension T must be positive\")\r\n    if not np.all((m == 0) | (m == 1)):\r\n        raise ValueError(\"m must contain only 0/1 values\")\r\n    m = m.astype(bool, copy=False)\r\n    if np.any(m & ~np.isfinite(x)):\r\n        raise ValueError(\"observed x values must be finite\")\r\n    w = float(w)\r\n    if not 0.0 <= w <= 1.0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"w must be in [0, 1]\")\r\n\r\n    transition_logits = np.asarray(parameters[\"transition_logits\"], dtype=np.float64)\r\n    initial_logits = np.asarray(parameters[\"initial_logits\"], dtype=np.float64)\r\n    mu = np.asarray(parameters[\"mu\"], dtype=np.float64)\r\n    c = np.asarray(parameters[\"c\"], dtype=np.float64)\r\n    u = np.asarray(parameters[\"u\"], dtype=np.float64)\r\n    log_sd = float(np.asarray(parameters[\"log_sd\"], dtype=np.float64).reshape(()))\r\n    n_states = mu.size\r\n    if (transition_logits.shape != (n_states, n_states)\r\n            or initial_logits.shape != (n_states,)\r\n            or c.shape != (n_states,) or u.shape != (n_states,)):\r\n        raise ValueError(\"filter parameters have inconsistent state dimensions\")\r\n    if not np.isfinite(log_sd):\r\n        raise ValueError(\"log_sd must be finite\")\r\n    return x, m, transition_logits, initial_logits, mu, log_sd, c, u, w\r\n\r\n\r\ndef numpy_forward(\r\n    x: np.ndarray,\r\n    m: np.ndarray,\r\n    parameters: Mapping[str, np.ndarray],\r\n    w: float = 1.0,\r\n) -> Tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray]:\r\n    \"\"\"Return filtered outcome probabilities and sequence log likelihoods.\r\n\r\n    ``x`` has shape [patients, T] with NaN at missing values; ``m`` contains\r\n    the corresponding 0/1 mask. The mask likelihood is multiplied by ``w``.\r\n    The transition is applied before each observation update, matching the\r\n    reference recursion in :mod:`bayes`.\r\n    \"\"\"\r\n    x, m, transition_logits, initial_logits, mu, log_sd, c, u, w = _check_inputs(\r\n        x, m, parameters, w\r\n    )\r\n    n_patients, n_steps = x.shape\r\n    log_transition = _log_softmax(transition_logits, axis=1)\r\n    log_belief = np.broadcast_to(_log_softmax(initial_logits), (n_patients, mu.size)).copy()\r\n    log_p_test = -np.logaddexp(0.0, -c)\r\n    log_not_test = -np.logaddexp(0.0, c)\r\n    sd = math.exp(log_sd)\r\n    log_normal_constant = -log_sd - 0.5 * math.log(2.0 * math.pi)\r\n    log_marginal = np.zeros(n_patients, dtype=np.float64)\r\n\r\n    for t in range(n_steps):\r\n        log_predicted = _logsumexp(\r\n            log_belief[:, :, None] + log_transition[None, :, :], axis=1\r\n        )\r\n        observed = m[:, t]\r\n        safe_x = np.where(observed, x[:, t], 0.0)\r\n        residual = (safe_x[:, None] - mu[None, :]) / sd\r\n        log_value = log_normal_constant - 0.5 * residual * residual\r\n        log_value = np.where(observed[:, None], log_value, 0.0)\r\n        log_test = w * (\r\n            observed[:, None] * log_p_test[None, :]\r\n            + (~observed)[:, None] * log_not_test[None, :]\r\n        )\r\n        log_unnormalized = log_predicted + log_value + log_test\r\n        normalizer = _logsumexp(log_unnormalized, axis=1)\r\n        log_belief = log_unnormalized - normalizer[:, None]\r\n        log_marginal += normalizer\r\n\r\n    posterior = np.exp(log_belief)\r\n    probabilities = posterior @ _sigmoid(u)\r\n    return probabilities, log_marginal\r\n\r\n\r\nif torch is not None:\r\n    class LatentFilter(nn.Module):\r\n        \"\"\"K-state differentiable filter with a deploy-time mask tempering knob.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n        def __init__(self, n_states: int = 32):\r\n            super().__init__()\r\n            if n_states < 2:\r\n                raise ValueError(\"n_states must be at least 2\")\r\n            self.n_states = int(n_states)\r\n            transition_logits = torch.zeros(n_states, n_states)\r\n            diagonal = torch.arange(n_states)\r\n            transition_logits[diagonal, diagonal] = 4.0\r\n            if n_states > 1:\r\n                transition_logits[diagonal[:-1], diagonal[1:]] = 2.0\r\n                transition_logits[diagonal[1:], diagonal[:-1]] = 2.0\r\n            means = torch.linspace(-2.5, 2.5, n_states)\r\n            initial_logits = -0.5 * means.square()\r\n            self.transition_logits = nn.Parameter(transition_logits)\r\n            self.initial_logits = nn.Parameter(initial_logits)\r\n            self.mu = nn.Parameter(means)\r\n            self.log_sd = nn.Parameter(torch.tensor(0.0))\r\n            self.c = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(n_states))\r\n            self.u = nn.Parameter(torch.zeros(n_states))\r\n\r\n        def forward(self, x, m, w: float = 1.0):\r\n            if not 0.0 <= float(w) <= 1.0:\r\n                raise ValueError(\"w must be in [0, 1]\")\r\n            mask = m.to(dtype=torch.bool)\r\n            log_transition = F.log_softmax(self.transition_logits, dim=1)\r\n            log_belief = F.log_softmax(self.initial_logits, dim=0).expand(x.shape[0], -1)\r\n            log_p_test = F.logsigmoid(self.c)\r\n            log_not_test = F.logsigmoid(-self.c)\r\n            sd = torch.exp(self.log_sd)\r\n            log_normal_constant = -self.log_sd - 0.5 * math.log(2.0 * math.pi)\r\n            log_marginal = torch.zeros(x.shape[0], dtype=x.dtype, device=x.device)\r\n\r\n            for t in range(x.shape[1]):\r\n                log_predicted = torch.logsumexp(\r\n                    log_belief.unsqueeze(2) + log_transition.unsqueeze(0), dim=1\r\n                )\r\n                observed = mask[:, t]\r\n                safe_x = torch.where(observed, x[:, t], torch.zeros_like(x[:, t]))\r\n                residual = (safe_x[:, None] - self.mu[None, :]) / sd\r\n                log_value = log_normal_constant - 0.5 * residual.square()\r\n                log_value = torch.where(observed[:, None], log_value, torch.zeros_like(log_value))\r\n                observed_float = observed.to(dtype=x.dtype)[:, None]\r\n                log_test = float(w) * (\r\n                    observed_float * log_p_test[None, :]\r\n                    + (1.0 - observed_float) * log_not_test[None, :]\r\n                )\r\n                log_unnormalized = log_predicted + log_value + log_test\r\n                normalizer = torch.logsumexp(log_unnormalized, dim=1)\r\n                log_belief = log_unnormalized - normalizer[:, None]\r\n                log_marginal = log_marginal + normalizer\r\n\r\n            posterior = torch.exp(log_belief)\r\n            probabilities = posterior @ torch.sigmoid(self.u)\r\n            return probabilities, log_marginal\r\n\r\n        def numpy_parameters(self) -> Dict[str, np.ndarray]:\r\n            return {\r\n                name: getattr(self, name).detach().cpu().numpy().copy()\r\n                for name in PARAMETER_NAMES\r\n            }\r\nelse:\r\n    class LatentFilter:  # pragma: no cover - only used to provide a clear runtime message\r\n        def __init__(self, *args, **kwargs):\r\n            raise ImportError(\"PyTorch is required to construct or train LatentFilter\")\r\n\r\n\r\ndef train_filter(\r\n    train_x: np.ndarray,\r\n    train_m: np.ndarray,\r\n    train_y: np.ndarray,\r\n    val_x: np.ndarray,\r\n    val_m: np.ndarray,\r\n    val_y: np.ndarray,\r\n    *,\r\n    n_states: int = 32,\r\n    max_epochs: int = 200,\r\n    batch_size: int = 512,\r\n    patience: int = 15,\r\n    seed: int = 31_337,\r\n) -> Tuple[LatentFilter, Dict[str, object]]:\r\n    \"\"\"Fit the filter on A data using BCE plus per-step marginal NLL.\"\"\"\r\n    if torch is None:\r\n        raise ImportError(\"PyTorch is required to train E5; NumPy forward is available\")\r\n    torch.set_num_threads(4)\r\n    torch.manual_seed(int(seed))\r\n    np.random.seed(int(seed) % (2**32 - 1))\r\n\r\n    def tensor_data(x, m, y):\r\n        return (\r\n            torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(x, dtype=np.float32)),\r\n            torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(m, dtype=np.float32)),\r\n            torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(y, dtype=np.float32)),\r\n        )\r\n\r\n    x_train, m_train, y_train = tensor_data(train_x, train_m, train_y)\r\n    x_val, m_val, y_val = tensor_data(val_x, val_m, val_y)\r\n    model = LatentFilter(n_states=n_states)\r\n    optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=1e-2)\r\n    n_train = y_train.shape[0]\r\n    best_loss = float(\"inf\")\r\n    best_state = None\r\n    best_epoch = 0\r\n    stale_epochs = 0\r\n    train_history = []\r\n    val_history = []\r\n    train_bce_history = []\r\n    val_bce_history = []\r\n    train_nll_history = []\r\n    val_nll_history = []\r\n\r\n    def evaluate(x, m, y):\r\n        model.eval()\r\n        total_loss = total_bce = total_nll = 0.0\r\n        with torch.no_grad():\r\n            for start in range(0, y.shape[0], batch_size):\r\n                stop = start + batch_size\r\n                probabilities, log_marginal = model(x[start:stop], m[start:stop], w=1.0)\r\n                bce = F.binary_cross_entropy(\r\n                    probabilities.clamp(1e-7, 1.0 - 1e-7), y[start:stop]\r\n                )\r\n                nll = -log_marginal.mean() / x.shape[1]\r\n                size = y[start:stop].shape[0]\r\n                total_bce += float(bce) * size\r\n                total_nll += float(nll) * size\r\n                total_loss += float(bce + nll) * size\r\n        count = int(y.shape[0])\r\n        return total_loss / count, total_bce / count, total_nll / count\r\n\r\n    for epoch in range(int(max_epochs)):\r\n        model.train()\r\n        permutation = torch.randperm(n_train)\r\n        total_loss = total_bce = total_nll = 0.0\r\n        for start in range(0, n_train, batch_size):\r\n            indices = permutation[start:start + batch_size]\r\n            xb, mb, yb = x_train[indices], m_train[indices], y_train[indices]\r\n            optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\r\n            probabilities, log_marginal = model(xb, mb, w=1.0)\r\n            bce = F.binary_cross_entropy(\r\n                probabilities.clamp(1e-7, 1.0 - 1e-7), yb\r\n            )\r\n            nll = -log_marginal.mean() / x_train.shape[1]\r\n            loss = bce + nll\r\n            loss.backward()\r\n            optimizer.step()\r\n            size = yb.shape[0]\r\n            total_bce += float(bce.detach()) * size\r\n            total_nll += float(nll.detach()) * size\r\n            total_loss += float(loss.detach()) * size\r\n        train_bce = total_bce / n_train\r\n        train_nll = total_nll / n_train\r\n        train_history.append(total_loss / n_train)\r\n        train_bce_history.append(train_bce)\r\n        train_nll_history.append(train_nll)\r\n\r\n        val_loss, val_bce, val_nll = evaluate(x_val, m_val, y_val)\r\n        val_history.append(val_loss)\r\n        val_bce_history.append(val_bce)\r\n        val_nll_history.append(val_nll)\r\n        if val_loss < best_loss - 1e-8:\r\n            best_loss = val_loss\r\n            best_epoch = epoch + 1\r\n            best_state = copy.deepcopy(model.state_dict())\r\n            stale_epochs = 0\r\n        else:\r\n            stale_epochs += 1\r\n        if stale_epochs >= patience:\r\n            break\r\n\r\n    if best_state is not None:\r\n        model.load_state_dict(best_state)\r\n    return model, {\r\n        \"best_epoch\": best_epoch,\r\n        \"epochs\": len(train_history),\r\n        \"train_loss\": train_history,\r\n        \"val_loss\": val_history,\r\n        \"train_bce\": train_bce_history,\r\n        \"val_bce\": val_bce_history,\r\n        \"train_nll_per_step\": train_nll_history,\r\n        \"val_nll_per_step\": val_nll_history,\r\n    }\r\n\r\n\r\ndef true_parameters(grid_size: int, sigma: float, policy) -> Dict[str, np.ndarray]:\r\n    \"\"\"Build exact simulator parameters on Bayes' discretized latent grid.\"\"\"\r\n    grid, initial, transition = bayes._grid_and_transition(int(grid_size))\r\n    future_sd = math.sqrt(1.0 - sim.RHO ** (2 * sim.HORIZON))\r\n    future_mean = sim.RHO ** sim.HORIZON * grid\r\n    event_probability = 1.0 - bayes._normal_cdf(\r\n        (sim.THRESHOLD - future_mean) / future_sd\r\n    )\r\n    event_probability = np.clip(event_probability, 1e-14, 1.0 - 1e-14)\r\n    return {\r\n        \"transition_logits\": np.log(transition),\r\n        \"initial_logits\": np.log(initial),\r\n        \"mu\": np.asarray(grid),\r\n        \"log_sd\": np.asarray(math.log(float(sigma))),\r\n        \"c\": policy.alpha + policy.gamma * np.asarray(grid),\r\n        \"u\": np.log(event_probability) - np.log1p(-event_probability),\r\n    }\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _atomic_npz(path: Path, arrays: Mapping[str, np.ndarray]) -> None:\r\n    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\r\n    descriptor, temp_path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=f\".{path.stem}.\", suffix=\".tmp\", dir=path.parent)\r\n    try:\r\n        with os.fdopen(descriptor, \"wb\") as handle:\r\n            np.savez_compressed(handle, **arrays)\r\n        os.replace(temp_path, path)\r\n    except BaseException:\r\n        try:\r\n            os.unlink(temp_path)\r\n        except FileNotFoundError:\r\n            pass\r\n        raise\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _atomic_json(path: Path, value: Mapping[str, object]) -> None:\r\n    path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\r\n    descriptor, temp_path = tempfile.mkstemp(prefix=f\".{path.stem}.\", suffix=\".tmp\", dir=path.parent)\r\n    try:\r\n        with os.fdopen(descriptor, \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\r\n            json.dump(value, handle, indent=2)\r\n        os.replace(temp_path, path)\r\n    except BaseException:\r\n        try:\r\n            os.unlink(temp_path)\r\n        except FileNotFoundError:\r\n            pass\r\n        raise\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _predict_family(model, test_observed: Mapping[str, Mapping[str, np.ndarray]], weights):\r\n    if torch is None:\r\n        raise ImportError(\"PyTorch is required to produce learned filter predictions\")\r\n    model.eval()\r\n    arrays: Dict[str, np.ndarray] = {}\r\n    with torch.no_grad():\r\n        for w in weights:\r\n            for policy_name, observed in test_observed.items():\r\n                x = np.asarray(observed[\"x\"], dtype=np.float32)\r\n                m = np.asarray(observed[\"m\"], dtype=np.float32)\r\n                chunks = []\r\n                for start in range(0, len(x), 512):\r\n                    tx = torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(x[start:start + 512]))\r\n                    tm = torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(m[start:start + 512]))\r\n                    probabilities, _ = model(tx, tm, w=float(w))\r\n                    chunks.append(probabilities.cpu().numpy())\r\n                arrays[f\"p_w{float(w):.2f}_{policy_name}\"] = np.concatenate(chunks).astype(\r\n                    np.float32, copy=False\r\n                )\r\n    return arrays\r\n\r\n\r\ndef run(smoke: bool = False) -> Path:\r\n    \"\"\"Train E5 on the paired E1 seed-0 cohorts and write the result shard.\"\"\"\r\n    n_train, n_val, n_test, max_epochs = (\r\n        (800, 200, 1000, 3) if smoke else (8000, 2000, 8000, 200)\r\n    )\r\n    out_root = run_grid._validate_out_root(ROOT / (\"results_smoke\" if smoke else \"results\"))\r\n    output_dir = out_root / \"E5\" / \"sigma1_gamma2\" / \"seed0\" / \"filter\"\r\n    expected = (output_dir / \"params.npz\", output_dir / \"probs.npz\", output_dir / \"meta.json\")\r\n    if all(path.exists() for path in expected):\r\n        print(f\"SKIP {output_dir} (complete output exists)\")\r\n        return output_dir\r\n    if torch is None:\r\n        raise ImportError(\"PyTorch is required to run latent_filter.py; NumPy checks still work\")\r\n\r\n    started = time.perf_counter()\r\n    train_policy, test_policies = run_grid._policy_setup(\"E1\", sigma=1.0, gamma=2.0)\r\n    train_latent_seed, val_latent_seed, test_latent_seed, train_obs_seed, val_obs_seed, test_obs_seed = (\r\n        run_grid._seeds_for(0)\r\n    )\r\n    train_observed, train_y = run_grid._observed_split(\r\n        n_train, train_latent_seed, train_obs_seed, train_policy, 1.0\r\n    )\r\n    val_observed, val_y = run_grid._observed_split(\r\n        n_val, val_latent_seed, val_obs_seed, train_policy, 1.0\r\n    )\r\n    test_latent = sim.simulate_latent(n_test, seed=test_latent_seed)\r\n    test_observed = {\r\n        name: sim.observe(test_latent, policy, sigma=1.0, seed=test_obs_seed)\r\n        for name, policy in test_policies.items()\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    model, fit_meta = train_filter(\r\n        train_observed[\"x\"], train_observed[\"m\"], train_y,\r\n        val_observed[\"x\"], val_observed[\"m\"], val_y,\r\n        n_states=32, max_epochs=max_epochs, batch_size=512, patience=15,\r\n        seed=31_337,\r\n    )\r\n    probabilities = _predict_family(model, test_observed, DEFAULT_WEIGHTS)\r\n    probabilities[\"y\"] = np.asarray(test_latent[\"y\"], dtype=np.int8)\r\n    meta = {\r\n        \"exp\": \"E5\",\r\n        \"config\": \"sigma1_gamma2\",\r\n        \"architecture\": \"latent_filter\",\r\n        \"seed\": 0,\r\n        \"n_train\": n_train,\r\n        \"n_val\": n_val,\r\n        \"n_test\": n_test,\r\n        \"best_epoch\": fit_meta[\"best_epoch\"],\r\n        \"epochs\": fit_meta[\"epochs\"],\r\n        \"train_loss\": fit_meta[\"train_loss\"],\r\n        \"val_loss\": fit_meta[\"val_loss\"],\r\n        \"train_bce\": fit_meta[\"train_bce\"],\r\n        \"val_bce\": fit_meta[\"val_bce\"],\r\n        \"train_nll_per_step\": fit_meta[\"train_nll_per_step\"],\r\n        \"val_nll_per_step\": fit_meta[\"val_nll_per_step\"],\r\n        \"wall_time_seconds\": time.perf_counter() - started,\r\n        \"test_policy_names\": list(test_policies),\r\n        \"weights\": DEFAULT_WEIGHTS.tolist(),\r\n    }\r\n    output_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\r\n    _atomic_npz(output_dir / \"params.npz\", model.numpy_parameters())\r\n    _atomic_npz(output_dir / \"probs.npz\", probabilities)\r\n    _atomic_json(output_dir / \"meta.json\", meta)\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"DONE {output_dir} best_epoch={fit_meta['best_epoch']} \"\r\n        f\"epochs={fit_meta['epochs']} wall={meta['wall_time_seconds']:.1f}s\"\r\n    )\r\n    return output_dir\r\n\r\n\r\ndef main(argv: Iterable[str] | None = None) -> None:\r\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--smoke\", action=\"store_true\")\r\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\r\n    run(smoke=args.smoke)\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    main()\r\n\"\"\"E12: re-learn the E5 testing head at B with and without outcomes.\r\n\r\nRun from the project folder with ``python e12_unlabelled_head.py``.  The\r\n``--smoke`` mode uses small cohorts and short refits; normal mode follows the\r\nsizes and optimisation steps in SPEC.md.\r\n\"\"\"\r\n\r\nfrom __future__ import annotations\r\n\r\nimport argparse\r\nimport multiprocessing as mp\r\nfrom pathlib import Path\r\nimport time\r\nfrom typing import Dict, Iterable, List, Mapping, Sequence, Tuple\r\n\r\nimport numpy as np\r\nimport pandas as pd\r\n\r\nimport bayes\r\nimport latent_filter\r\nimport run_grid\r\nimport sim\r\n\r\n\r\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\r\nPARAMS_PATH = ROOT / \"results\" / \"E5\" / \"sigma1_gamma2\" / \"seed0\" / \"filter\" / \"params.npz\"\r\nSIGMA = 1.0\r\nPOLICY_NAMES = (\"B_less\", \"B_more\", \"B_match\", \"B_dense\")\r\nPOOL_SEED_BASE = 1_300_000\r\nTEST_OBS_SEED_BASE = 1_200_000\r\nRUN_COLUMNS = (\r\n    \"policy\", \"method\", \"size_type\", \"size\", \"draw\", \"brier\", \"log_loss\",\r\n    \"auroc\", \"regret\", \"w\", \"wall_time_seconds\",\r\n)\r\nCURVE_COLUMNS = (\r\n    \"policy\", \"n_U\", \"draw\", \"state_index\", \"mu\", \"refitted_probability\",\r\n    \"true_testing_probability\",\r\n)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _policies() -> Dict[str, sim.Policy]:\r\n    \"\"\"Build the four target policies specified for E12.\"\"\"\r\n    return {\r\n        \"B_less\": sim.informative(gamma=1.0, rate=0.3),\r\n        \"B_more\": sim.informative(gamma=3.5, rate=0.3),\r\n        \"B_match\": sim.uniform(rate=0.3),\r\n        \"B_dense\": sim.uniform(rate=0.6),\r\n    }\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _load_parameters(path: Path | str) -> Dict[str, np.ndarray]:\r\n    with np.load(path, allow_pickle=False) as stored:\r\n        return {name: np.asarray(stored[name]).copy() for name in latent_filter.PARAMETER_NAMES}\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _sigmoid(values: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\r\n    values = np.asarray(values, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    result = np.empty_like(values)\r\n    positive = values >= 0.0\r\n    result[positive] = 1.0 / (1.0 + np.exp(-values[positive]))\r\n    exp_values = np.exp(values[~positive])\r\n    result[~positive] = exp_values / (1.0 + exp_values)\r\n    return result\r\n\r\n\r\ndef auroc_rank(y_true: np.ndarray, scores: np.ndarray) -> float:\r\n    \"\"\"Compute AUROC from average ranks, assigning average ranks to ties.\"\"\"\r\n    y = np.asarray(y_true, dtype=bool).reshape(-1)\r\n    score = np.asarray(scores, dtype=np.float64).reshape(-1)\r\n    if y.size != score.size:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"y_true and scores must have the same length\")\r\n    positives = int(y.sum())\r\n    negatives = y.size - positives\r\n    if positives == 0 or negatives == 0:\r\n        return float(\"nan\")\r\n\r\n    order = np.argsort(score, kind=\"mergesort\")\r\n    ordered = score[order]\r\n    ranks = np.empty(y.size, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    start = 0\r\n    while start < y.size:\r\n        stop = start + 1\r\n        while stop < y.size and ordered[stop] == ordered[start]:\r\n            stop += 1\r\n        # Ranks are one-based; all tied items receive their average rank.\r\n        ranks[order[start:stop]] = 0.5 * ((start + 1) + stop)\r\n        start = stop\r\n    positive_rank_sum = float(ranks[y].sum())\r\n    return (positive_rank_sum - positives * (positives + 1) / 2.0) / (positives * negatives)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _metrics(y: np.ndarray, probability: np.ndarray, oracle_brier: float) -> Dict[str, float]:\r\n    y = np.asarray(y, dtype=np.float64).reshape(-1)\r\n    probability = np.asarray(probability, dtype=np.float64).reshape(-1)\r\n    if y.shape != probability.shape:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"prediction and outcome arrays must have matching shapes\")\r\n    clipped = np.clip(probability, 1e-7, 1.0 - 1e-7)\r\n    brier = float(np.mean((probability - y) ** 2))\r\n    log_loss = float(-np.mean(y * np.log(clipped) + (1.0 - y) * np.log1p(-clipped)))\r\n    return {\r\n        \"brier\": brier,\r\n        \"log_loss\": log_loss,\r\n        \"auroc\": auroc_rank(y, probability),\r\n        \"regret\": brier - float(oracle_brier),\r\n    }\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _pool_seeds(policy_index: int, size_type: str, size_index: int, draw: int) -> Tuple[int, int, int]:\r\n    \"\"\"Return disjoint latent, observation, and minibatch seeds for one pool.\"\"\"\r\n    type_offset = 0 if size_type == \"n_L\" else 25_000\r\n    latent_seed = POOL_SEED_BASE + policy_index * 50_000 + type_offset + size_index * 1_000 + draw * 3\r\n    return latent_seed, latent_seed + 1, latent_seed + 2\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _observe_pool(n: int, policy: sim.Policy, latent_seed: int, obs_seed: int):\r\n    latent = sim.simulate_latent(n, seed=latent_seed)\r\n    observed = sim.observe(latent, policy, sigma=SIGMA, seed=obs_seed)\r\n    return observed, np.asarray(latent[\"y\"], dtype=np.float32)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _choose_dial_weight(x: np.ndarray, m: np.ndarray, y: np.ndarray,\r\n                        parameters: Mapping[str, np.ndarray]) -> float:\r\n    \"\"\"Choose the labelled run-in log-loss minimiser, breaking ties toward 1.\"\"\"\r\n    weights = np.round(np.arange(0.0, 1.0001, 0.05), 2)\r\n    losses = []\r\n    for weight in weights:\r\n        probability, _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(x, m, parameters, w=float(weight))\r\n        clipped = np.clip(probability, 1e-7, 1.0 - 1e-7)\r\n        loss = -np.mean(y * np.log(clipped) + (1.0 - y) * np.log1p(-clipped))\r\n        losses.append(float(loss))\r\n    best_loss = min(losses)\r\n    return float(max(weight for weight, loss in zip(weights, losses) if loss == best_loss))\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _refit_testing_head(\r\n    observed: Mapping[str, np.ndarray],\r\n    labels: np.ndarray | None,\r\n    parameters: Mapping[str, np.ndarray],\r\n    *,\r\n    batch_seed: int,\r\n    steps: int,\r\n    lr: float = 1e-2,\r\n) -> Dict[str, np.ndarray]:\r\n    \"\"\"Refit c only, using the E5 likelihood and optional outcome BCE.\"\"\"\r\n    try:\r\n        import torch\r\n        from torch.nn import functional as F\r\n    except ImportError as exc:  # Keep NumPy checks usable without PyTorch.\r\n        raise ImportError(\"PyTorch is required for the E12 head refits\") from exc\r\n\r\n    torch.set_num_threads(1)\r\n    torch.manual_seed(int(batch_seed))\r\n    model = latent_filter.LatentFilter(n_states=int(np.asarray(parameters[\"mu\"]).size))\r\n    with torch.no_grad():\r\n        for name in latent_filter.PARAMETER_NAMES:\r\n            tensor = torch.as_tensor(np.asarray(parameters[name]), dtype=getattr(model, name).dtype)\r\n            getattr(model, name).copy_(tensor)\r\n\r\n    for name, parameter in model.named_parameters():\r\n        parameter.requires_grad_(name == \"c\")\r\n    optimizer = torch.optim.Adam([model.c], lr=float(lr))\r\n\r\n    x = torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(observed[\"x\"], dtype=np.float32))\r\n    m = torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(observed[\"m\"], dtype=np.float32))\r\n    y_tensor = None if labels is None else torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(labels, dtype=np.float32))\r\n    n = int(x.shape[0])\r\n    if n == 0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"cannot refit the testing head on an empty pool\")\r\n    generator = torch.Generator(device=\"cpu\")\r\n    generator.manual_seed(int(batch_seed))\r\n    model.train()\r\n\r\n    for _ in range(int(steps)):\r\n        # Sampling with replacement keeps every optimizer step at 256 records,\r\n        # including the small labelled run-ins in E12.\r\n        indices = torch.randint(0, n, (256,), generator=generator)\r\n        xb = x.index_select(0, indices)\r\n        mb = m.index_select(0, indices)\r\n        probabilities, log_marginal = model(xb, mb, w=1.0)\r\n        nll_per_step = -log_marginal.mean() / x.shape[1]\r\n        loss = nll_per_step\r\n        if y_tensor is not None:\r\n            yb = y_tensor.index_select(0, indices)\r\n            bce = F.binary_cross_entropy(probabilities.clamp(1e-7, 1.0 - 1e-7), yb)\r\n            loss = bce + nll_per_step\r\n        optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\r\n        loss.backward()\r\n        optimizer.step()\r\n\r\n    return model.numpy_parameters()\r\n\r\n\r\n_WORKER_TEST_OBS: Dict[str, Mapping[str, np.ndarray]] = {}\r\n_WORKER_TEST_Y: np.ndarray | None = None\r\n_WORKER_PARAMS_PATH: str | None = None\r\n_WORKER_POLICIES: Dict[str, sim.Policy] = {}\r\n_WORKER_ORACLE_BRIER: Dict[str, float] = {}\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _init_worker(\r\n    test_observations: Dict[str, Mapping[str, np.ndarray]],\r\n    test_y: np.ndarray,\r\n    params_path: str,\r\n    oracle_brier: Dict[str, float],\r\n) -> None:\r\n    global _WORKER_TEST_OBS, _WORKER_TEST_Y, _WORKER_PARAMS_PATH, _WORKER_POLICIES\r\n    global _WORKER_ORACLE_BRIER\r\n    _WORKER_TEST_OBS = test_observations\r\n    _WORKER_TEST_Y = test_y\r\n    _WORKER_PARAMS_PATH = params_path\r\n    _WORKER_POLICIES = _policies()\r\n    _WORKER_ORACLE_BRIER = oracle_brier\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _run_refit_job(task: Mapping[str, object]) -> Dict[str, object]:\r\n    \"\"\"Run one policy/size/draw job and return metric rows plus any curve.\"\"\"\r\n    if _WORKER_TEST_Y is None or _WORKER_PARAMS_PATH is None:\r\n        raise RuntimeError(\"worker was not initialised\")\r\n    started = time.perf_counter()\r\n    policy_name = str(task[\"policy\"])\r\n    policy_index = int(task[\"policy_index\"])\r\n    size_type = str(task[\"size_type\"])\r\n    size_index = int(task[\"size_index\"])\r\n    size = int(task[\"size\"])\r\n    draw = int(task[\"draw\"])\r\n    policy = _WORKER_POLICIES[policy_name]\r\n    latent_seed, obs_seed, batch_seed = _pool_seeds(policy_index, size_type, size_index, draw)\r\n    pool, pool_y = _observe_pool(size, policy, latent_seed, obs_seed)\r\n    test_observed = _WORKER_TEST_OBS[policy_name]\r\n    test_y = _WORKER_TEST_Y\r\n    parameters = _load_parameters(_WORKER_PARAMS_PATH)\r\n    oracle_brier = _WORKER_ORACLE_BRIER[policy_name]\r\n    rows: List[Dict[str, object]] = []\r\n    curves: List[Dict[str, object]] = []\r\n\r\n    if size_type == \"n_L\":\r\n        method_started = time.perf_counter()\r\n        dial_w = _choose_dial_weight(pool[\"x\"], pool[\"m\"], pool_y, parameters)\r\n        dial_probability, _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(\r\n            test_observed[\"x\"], test_observed[\"m\"], parameters, w=dial_w\r\n        )\r\n        dial_elapsed = time.perf_counter() - method_started\r\n        rows.append({\r\n            \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n            \"method\": \"dial_L\",\r\n            \"size_type\": size_type,\r\n            \"size\": size,\r\n            \"draw\": draw,\r\n            **_metrics(test_y, dial_probability, oracle_brier),\r\n            \"w\": dial_w,\r\n            \"wall_time_seconds\": dial_elapsed,\r\n        })\r\n\r\n        method_started = time.perf_counter()\r\n        refitted = _refit_testing_head(\r\n            pool, pool_y, parameters, batch_seed=batch_seed, steps=int(task[\"steps\"]),\r\n            lr=float(task[\"lr\"])\r\n        )\r\n        head_probability, _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(\r\n            test_observed[\"x\"], test_observed[\"m\"], refitted, w=1.0\r\n        )\r\n        head_elapsed = time.perf_counter() - method_started\r\n        rows.append({\r\n            \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n            \"method\": \"head_L\",\r\n            \"size_type\": size_type,\r\n            \"size\": size,\r\n            \"draw\": draw,\r\n            **_metrics(test_y, head_probability, oracle_brier),\r\n            \"w\": 1.0,\r\n            \"wall_time_seconds\": head_elapsed,\r\n        })\r\n    elif size_type == \"n_U\":\r\n        method_started = time.perf_counter()\r\n        refitted = _refit_testing_head(\r\n            pool, None, parameters, batch_seed=batch_seed, steps=int(task[\"steps\"]),\r\n            lr=float(task[\"lr\"])\r\n        )\r\n        head_probability, _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(\r\n            test_observed[\"x\"], test_observed[\"m\"], refitted, w=1.0\r\n        )\r\n        head_elapsed = time.perf_counter() - method_started\r\n        rows.append({\r\n            \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n            \"method\": \"head_U\",\r\n            \"size_type\": size_type,\r\n            \"size\": size,\r\n            \"draw\": draw,\r\n            **_metrics(test_y, head_probability, oracle_brier),\r\n            \"w\": 1.0,\r\n            \"wall_time_seconds\": head_elapsed,\r\n        })\r\n        if size == int(task[\"curve_size\"]) and draw == 0:\r\n            mu = np.asarray(refitted[\"mu\"], dtype=np.float64)\r\n            refitted_probability = _sigmoid(np.asarray(refitted[\"c\"], dtype=np.float64))\r\n            if policy.name.startswith(\"informative\"):\r\n                true_probability = _sigmoid(policy.alpha + policy.gamma * mu)\r\n            else:\r\n                true_probability = np.full(mu.shape, policy.rate, dtype=np.float64)\r\n            curves = [\r\n                {\r\n                    \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n                    \"n_U\": size,\r\n                    \"draw\": draw,\r\n                    \"state_index\": index,\r\n                    \"mu\": float(mu[index]),\r\n                    \"refitted_probability\": float(refitted_probability[index]),\r\n                    \"true_testing_probability\": float(true_probability[index]),\r\n                }\r\n                for index in range(mu.size)\r\n            ]\r\n    else:\r\n        raise ValueError(f\"unknown size_type: {size_type}\")\r\n\r\n    return {\r\n        \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n        \"size_type\": size_type,\r\n        \"size\": size,\r\n        \"draw\": draw,\r\n        \"elapsed_seconds\": time.perf_counter() - started,\r\n        \"rows\": rows,\r\n        \"curves\": curves,\r\n    }\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _baseline_rows(\r\n    test_observations: Mapping[str, Mapping[str, np.ndarray]],\r\n    test_y: np.ndarray,\r\n    parameters: Mapping[str, np.ndarray],\r\n    policies: Mapping[str, sim.Policy],\r\n    size_specs: Sequence[Tuple[str, Sequence[int]]],\r\n    draws: int,\r\n) -> Tuple[List[Dict[str, object]], Dict[str, float]]:\r\n    \"\"\"Compute keep/drop/oracle once per policy, then attach rows to each size.\"\"\"\r\n    rows: List[Dict[str, object]] = []\r\n    oracle_briers: Dict[str, float] = {}\r\n    for policy_name in POLICY_NAMES:\r\n        observed = test_observations[policy_name]\r\n        policy = policies[policy_name]\r\n        baseline_predictions = {}\r\n        timings = {}\r\n        for method, weight in ((\"keep\", 1.0), (\"drop\", 0.0)):\r\n            started = time.perf_counter()\r\n            baseline_predictions[method], _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(\r\n                observed[\"x\"], observed[\"m\"], parameters, w=weight\r\n            )\r\n            timings[method] = time.perf_counter() - started\r\n        started = time.perf_counter()\r\n        baseline_predictions[\"oracle\"] = bayes.bayes_predict(\r\n            observed[\"x\"], observed[\"m\"], SIGMA, policy\r\n        )\r\n        timings[\"oracle\"] = time.perf_counter() - started\r\n        baseline_metrics = {\r\n            method: _metrics(test_y, probability, 0.0)\r\n            for method, probability in baseline_predictions.items()\r\n        }\r\n        oracle_brier = baseline_metrics[\"oracle\"][\"brier\"]\r\n        oracle_briers[policy_name] = oracle_brier\r\n        for size_type, sizes in size_specs:\r\n            for size in sizes:\r\n                for draw in range(draws):\r\n                    for method in (\"keep\", \"drop\", \"oracle\"):\r\n                        weight = {\"keep\": 1.0, \"drop\": 0.0, \"oracle\": float(\"nan\")}[method]\r\n                        rows.append({\r\n                            \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n                            \"method\": method,\r\n                            \"size_type\": size_type,\r\n                            \"size\": int(size),\r\n                            \"draw\": draw,\r\n                            **{\r\n                                **baseline_metrics[method],\r\n                                \"regret\": baseline_metrics[method][\"brier\"] - oracle_brier,\r\n                            },\r\n                            \"w\": weight,\r\n                            \"wall_time_seconds\": timings[method],\r\n                        })\r\n    return rows, oracle_briers\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _write_outputs(rows: Sequence[Mapping[str, object]], curves: Sequence[Mapping[str, object]],\r\n                   *, smoke: bool, tag: str = \"\") -> Tuple[Path, Path, Path]:\r\n    analysis_dir = ROOT / \"analysis\"\r\n    analysis_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\r\n    prefix = (\"e12_smoke_\" if smoke else \"e12_\") + (f\"{tag}_\" if tag else \"\")\r\n    runs_path = analysis_dir / f\"{prefix}runs.csv\"\r\n    summary_path = analysis_dir / f\"{prefix}summary.csv\"\r\n    curves_path = analysis_dir / f\"{prefix}c_curves.csv\"\r\n\r\n    runs = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=RUN_COLUMNS)\r\n    runs = runs.sort_values(\r\n        [\"policy\", \"size_type\", \"size\", \"draw\", \"method\"], kind=\"mergesort\"\r\n    ).reset_index(drop=True)\r\n    runs.to_csv(runs_path, index=False)\r\n\r\n    group_columns = [\"policy\", \"method\", \"size_type\", \"size\"]\r\n    summary_metrics = [\"brier\", \"log_loss\", \"auroc\", \"regret\", \"w\", \"wall_time_seconds\"]\r\n    summary = runs.groupby(group_columns, dropna=False)[summary_metrics].agg([\"mean\", \"min\", \"max\"])\r\n    summary.columns = [f\"{metric}_{stat}\" for metric, stat in summary.columns]\r\n    summary = summary.reset_index().sort_values(group_columns, kind=\"mergesort\")\r\n    summary.to_csv(summary_path, index=False)\r\n\r\n    pd.DataFrame(curves, columns=CURVE_COLUMNS).sort_values(\r\n        [\"policy\", \"state_index\"], kind=\"mergesort\"\r\n    ).to_csv(curves_path, index=False)\r\n    return runs_path, summary_path, curves_path\r\n\r\n\r\ndef run(*, workers: int = 4, smoke: bool = False, steps: int | None = None,\r\n        lr: float = 1e-2, tag: str = \"\") -> Tuple[Path, Path, Path]:\r\n    if workers < 1:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"--workers must be at least 1\")\r\n    if not PARAMS_PATH.exists():\r\n        raise FileNotFoundError(f\"E5 parameter file not found: {PARAMS_PATH}\")\r\n    parameters = _load_parameters(PARAMS_PATH)\r\n    policies = _policies()\r\n    n_test = 500 if smoke else 8_000\r\n    labelled_sizes = (30,) if smoke else (30, 125, 500)\r\n    unlabelled_sizes = (100,) if smoke else (200, 2_000)\r\n    draws = 1 if smoke else 3\r\n    if steps is None:\r\n        steps = 2 if smoke else 80\r\n    size_specs = ((\"n_L\", labelled_sizes), (\"n_U\", unlabelled_sizes))\r\n\r\n    # E5's test latent cohort is reused, while every B policy gets a fresh,\r\n    # fixed observation draw as specified for E12.\r\n    test_latent_seed = run_grid._seeds_for(0)[2]\r\n    test_latent = sim.simulate_latent(n_test, seed=test_latent_seed)\r\n    test_y = np.asarray(test_latent[\"y\"], dtype=np.float32)\r\n    test_observations = {\r\n        policy_name: sim.observe(\r\n            test_latent,\r\n            policies[policy_name],\r\n            sigma=SIGMA,\r\n            seed=TEST_OBS_SEED_BASE + policy_index,\r\n        )\r\n        for policy_index, policy_name in enumerate(POLICY_NAMES)\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    rows, oracle_briers = _baseline_rows(\r\n        test_observations, test_y, parameters, policies, size_specs, draws\r\n    )\r\n    tasks = []\r\n    max_unlabelled_size = max(unlabelled_sizes)\r\n    for policy_index, policy_name in enumerate(POLICY_NAMES):\r\n        for size_type, sizes in size_specs:\r\n            for size_index, size in enumerate(sizes):\r\n                for draw in range(draws):\r\n                    tasks.append({\r\n                        \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n                        \"policy_index\": policy_index,\r\n                        \"size_type\": size_type,\r\n                        \"size_index\": size_index,\r\n                        \"size\": int(size),\r\n                        \"draw\": draw,\r\n                        \"steps\": steps,\r\n                        \"lr\": lr,\r\n                        \"curve_size\": max_unlabelled_size,\r\n                    })\r\n\r\n    context = mp.get_context(\"spawn\")\r\n    curves: List[Dict[str, object]] = []\r\n    with context.Pool(\r\n        processes=workers,\r\n        initializer=_init_worker,\r\n        initargs=(test_observations, test_y, str(PARAMS_PATH), oracle_briers),\r\n    ) as pool:\r\n        for result in pool.imap_unordered(_run_refit_job, tasks, chunksize=1):\r\n            rows.extend(result[\"rows\"])\r\n            curves.extend(result[\"curves\"])\r\n            print(\r\n                f\"DONE {result['policy']} {result['size_type']}={result['size']} \"\r\n                f\"draw={result['draw']} wall={result['elapsed_seconds']:.1f}s\",\r\n                flush=True,\r\n            )\r\n\r\n    outputs = _write_outputs(rows, curves, smoke=smoke, tag=tag)\r\n    for path in outputs:\r\n        print(f\"WROTE {path.relative_to(ROOT)}\", flush=True)\r\n    return outputs\r\n\r\n\r\ndef main(argv: Iterable[str] | None = None) -> None:\r\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--workers\", type=int, default=4)\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--smoke\", action=\"store_true\")\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--steps\", type=int, default=None, help=\"Adam steps per refit (default 80; smoke 2)\")\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--lr\", type=float, default=1e-2)\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--tag\", default=\"\", help=\"suffix for output file names\")\r\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\r\n    run(workers=args.workers, smoke=args.smoke, steps=args.steps, lr=args.lr, tag=args.tag)\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    mp.freeze_support()\r\n    main()\r\n\"\"\"E13: compare adaptation rungs as a deployment run-in grows week by week.\r\n\r\nRun from the project folder with ``python e13_rungs_over_time.py``.  The\r\n``--smoke`` option uses short training/refits and writes separate smoke files.\r\n\"\"\"\r\n\r\nfrom __future__ import annotations\r\n\r\nimport argparse\r\nimport json\r\nimport multiprocessing as mp\r\nfrom pathlib import Path\r\nimport time\r\nfrom typing import Dict, Iterable, List, Mapping, Sequence, Tuple\r\n\r\nimport numpy as np\r\nimport pandas as pd\r\n\r\nimport bayes\r\nimport sim\r\n\r\n# bayes imports THRESHOLD by value, so patch both modules at import time.  This\r\n# module is imported afresh by spawned workers, which keeps their outcome rule\r\n# aligned with the A model and the main process.\r\nsim.THRESHOLD = 2.05\r\nbayes.THRESHOLD = 2.05\r\n\r\nimport e12_unlabelled_head as e12\r\nimport latent_filter\r\n\r\n\r\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\r\nSIGMA = 1.0\r\nWEEKS = (1, 2, 4, 8, 13, 26, 52, 104)\r\nSMOKE_WEEKS = (1, 4)\r\nN_PER_WEEK = 25\r\nSTREAM_N = 2_600\r\nPOLICY_NAMES = (\"B_less\", \"B_more\", \"B_match\", \"B_dense\")\r\n\r\n# All E13 seed ranges are separate from the E5/E12 seeds and from one another.\r\nA_TRAIN_LATENT_SEED = 1_500_000\r\nA_VAL_LATENT_SEED = 1_501_000\r\nA_TRAIN_OBS_SEED = 1_502_000\r\nA_VAL_OBS_SEED = 1_503_000\r\nA_MODEL_SEED = 1_504_337\r\nTEST_LATENT_SEED = 1_550_000\r\nTEST_OBS_SEED_BASE = 1_560_000\r\nSTREAM_SEED_BASE = 1_600_000\r\n\r\nRUN_COLUMNS = (\r\n    \"policy\", \"method\", \"week\", \"n_patients\", \"n_cases\", \"draw\",\r\n    \"brier\", \"log_loss\", \"auroc\", \"regret\", \"w\", \"wall_time_seconds\",\r\n)\r\nSUMMARY_METRICS = (\r\n    \"n_patients\", \"n_cases\", \"brier\", \"log_loss\", \"auroc\", \"regret\",\r\n    \"w\", \"wall_time_seconds\",\r\n)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _a_model_paths(smoke: bool) -> Tuple[Path, Path]:\r\n    folder = ROOT / (\"results_smoke\" if smoke else \"results\") / \"E13\" / \"a_model\"\r\n    return folder / \"params.npz\", folder / \"meta.json\"\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _observe_cohort(n: int, latent_seed: int, obs_seed: int, policy):\r\n    latent = sim.simulate_latent(n, seed=latent_seed)\r\n    observed = sim.observe(latent, policy, sigma=SIGMA, seed=obs_seed)\r\n    return observed, np.asarray(latent[\"y\"], dtype=np.float32)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _train_or_load_a_model(smoke: bool) -> Tuple[Path, Dict[str, object]]:\r\n    n_train, n_val, max_epochs = (1_000, 300, 3) if smoke else (16_000, 4_000, 200)\r\n    params_path, meta_path = _a_model_paths(smoke)\r\n    train_policy = sim.informative(gamma=2.0, rate=0.3)\r\n\r\n    train_latent = sim.simulate_latent(n_train, seed=A_TRAIN_LATENT_SEED)\r\n    train_y = np.asarray(train_latent[\"y\"], dtype=np.float32)\r\n    training_rate = float(train_y.mean())\r\n    assert 0.01 <= training_rate <= 0.03, (\r\n        f\"E13 A training outcome rate must be 1%-3%; got {training_rate:.4%}\"\r\n    )\r\n\r\n    if params_path.exists() and meta_path.exists():\r\n        with meta_path.open(\"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\r\n            metadata = json.load(handle)\r\n        print(f\"SKIP A model training (cached at {params_path.parent})\", flush=True)\r\n        return params_path, metadata\r\n\r\n    if latent_filter.torch is None:\r\n        raise ImportError(\"PyTorch is required to train the E13 A filter\")\r\n    started = time.perf_counter()\r\n    train_observed = sim.observe(\r\n        train_latent, train_policy, sigma=SIGMA, seed=A_TRAIN_OBS_SEED\r\n    )\r\n    val_observed, val_y = _observe_cohort(\r\n        n_val, A_VAL_LATENT_SEED, A_VAL_OBS_SEED, train_policy\r\n    )\r\n    model, fit_meta = latent_filter.train_filter(\r\n        train_observed[\"x\"], train_observed[\"m\"], train_y,\r\n        val_observed[\"x\"], val_observed[\"m\"], val_y,\r\n        n_states=32,\r\n        max_epochs=max_epochs,\r\n        batch_size=512,\r\n        patience=15,\r\n        seed=A_MODEL_SEED,\r\n    )\r\n    metadata: Dict[str, object] = {\r\n        \"experiment\": \"E13\",\r\n        \"model\": \"latent_filter\",\r\n        \"sigma\": SIGMA,\r\n        \"training_policy\": \"informative_gamma2_rate0.3\",\r\n        \"threshold\": 2.05,\r\n        \"n_train\": n_train,\r\n        \"n_val\": n_val,\r\n        \"n_train_cases\": int(train_y.sum()),\r\n        \"train_outcome_rate\": training_rate,\r\n        \"n_val_cases\": int(np.asarray(val_y).sum()),\r\n        \"train_latent_seed\": A_TRAIN_LATENT_SEED,\r\n        \"val_latent_seed\": A_VAL_LATENT_SEED,\r\n        \"train_observation_seed\": A_TRAIN_OBS_SEED,\r\n        \"val_observation_seed\": A_VAL_OBS_SEED,\r\n        \"model_seed\": A_MODEL_SEED,\r\n        \"best_epoch\": fit_meta[\"best_epoch\"],\r\n        \"epochs\": fit_meta[\"epochs\"],\r\n        \"train_loss\": fit_meta[\"train_loss\"],\r\n        \"val_loss\": fit_meta[\"val_loss\"],\r\n        \"train_bce\": fit_meta[\"train_bce\"],\r\n        \"val_bce\": fit_meta[\"val_bce\"],\r\n        \"train_nll_per_step\": fit_meta[\"train_nll_per_step\"],\r\n        \"val_nll_per_step\": fit_meta[\"val_nll_per_step\"],\r\n        \"wall_time_seconds\": time.perf_counter() - started,\r\n    }\r\n    latent_filter._atomic_npz(params_path, model.numpy_parameters())\r\n    latent_filter._atomic_json(meta_path, metadata)\r\n    print(\r\n        f\"DONE A model training at {params_path.parent} \"\r\n        f\"rate={training_rate:.3%} best_epoch={fit_meta['best_epoch']} \"\r\n        f\"wall={metadata['wall_time_seconds']:.1f}s\",\r\n        flush=True,\r\n    )\r\n    return params_path, metadata\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _stream_seeds(policy_index: int, draw: int) -> Tuple[int, int, int]:\r\n    base = STREAM_SEED_BASE + policy_index * 10_000 + draw * 100\r\n    return base, base + 1, base + 2\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _choose_penalized_weight(\r\n    x: np.ndarray,\r\n    m: np.ndarray,\r\n    y: np.ndarray,\r\n    parameters: Mapping[str, np.ndarray],\r\n) -> float:\r\n    \"\"\"Minimize summed labelled log loss plus the E9 quadratic penalty.\"\"\"\r\n    if int(np.asarray(y).sum()) == 0:\r\n        return 1.0\r\n    weights = np.round(np.arange(0.0, 1.0001, 0.05), 2)\r\n    losses: List[float] = []\r\n    labels = np.asarray(y, dtype=np.float64)\r\n    for weight in weights:\r\n        probability, _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(x, m, parameters, w=float(weight))\r\n        clipped = np.clip(probability, 1e-7, 1.0 - 1e-7)\r\n        log_loss_sum = -np.sum(\r\n            labels * np.log(clipped) + (1.0 - labels) * np.log1p(-clipped)\r\n        )\r\n        losses.append(float(log_loss_sum + 2.0 * (float(weight) - 1.0) ** 2))\r\n    best_loss = min(losses)\r\n    return float(max(weight for weight, loss in zip(weights, losses) if loss == best_loss))\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _finetune(\r\n    observed: Mapping[str, np.ndarray],\r\n    labels: np.ndarray,\r\n    parameters: Mapping[str, np.ndarray],\r\n    *,\r\n    batch_seed: int,\r\n    steps: int,\r\n    lr: float,\r\n) -> Dict[str, np.ndarray]:\r\n    \"\"\"Fine-tune every A parameter with the E5 objective and replacement batches.\"\"\"\r\n    if int(np.asarray(labels).sum()) == 0:\r\n        return {name: np.asarray(parameters[name]).copy() for name in latent_filter.PARAMETER_NAMES}\r\n    try:\r\n        import torch\r\n        from torch.nn import functional as F\r\n    except ImportError as exc:\r\n        raise ImportError(\"PyTorch is required for E13 fine-tuning\") from exc\r\n\r\n    torch.set_num_threads(1)\r\n    torch.manual_seed(int(batch_seed))\r\n    model = latent_filter.LatentFilter(n_states=int(np.asarray(parameters[\"mu\"]).size))\r\n    with torch.no_grad():\r\n        for name in latent_filter.PARAMETER_NAMES:\r\n            tensor = torch.as_tensor(\r\n                np.asarray(parameters[name]), dtype=getattr(model, name).dtype\r\n            )\r\n            getattr(model, name).copy_(tensor)\r\n    optimizer = torch.optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=float(lr))\r\n    x = torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(observed[\"x\"], dtype=np.float32))\r\n    m = torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(observed[\"m\"], dtype=np.float32))\r\n    y = torch.from_numpy(np.ascontiguousarray(labels, dtype=np.float32))\r\n    n = int(x.shape[0])\r\n    generator = torch.Generator(device=\"cpu\")\r\n    generator.manual_seed(int(batch_seed))\r\n    model.train()\r\n\r\n    for _ in range(int(steps)):\r\n        indices = torch.randint(0, n, (256,), generator=generator)\r\n        xb = x.index_select(0, indices)\r\n        mb = m.index_select(0, indices)\r\n        yb = y.index_select(0, indices)\r\n        probability, log_marginal = model(xb, mb, w=1.0)\r\n        bce = F.binary_cross_entropy(\r\n            probability.clamp(1e-7, 1.0 - 1e-7), yb\r\n        )\r\n        nll_per_step = -log_marginal.mean() / x.shape[1]\r\n        loss = bce + nll_per_step\r\n        optimizer.zero_grad(set_to_none=True)\r\n        loss.backward()\r\n        optimizer.step()\r\n    return model.numpy_parameters()\r\n\r\n\r\n_WORKER_TEST_OBS: Dict[str, Mapping[str, np.ndarray]] = {}\r\n_WORKER_TEST_Y: np.ndarray | None = None\r\n_WORKER_ORACLE: Dict[str, np.ndarray] = {}\r\n_WORKER_ORACLE_BRIER: Dict[str, float] = {}\r\n_WORKER_PARAMS: Dict[str, np.ndarray] = {}\r\n_WORKER_POLICIES: Dict[str, sim.Policy] = {}\r\n_WORKER_LR = 0.05\r\n_WORKER_STEPS = 160\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _init_worker(\r\n    test_observations: Dict[str, Mapping[str, np.ndarray]],\r\n    test_y: np.ndarray,\r\n    oracle_predictions: Dict[str, np.ndarray],\r\n    params_path: str,\r\n    lr: float,\r\n    steps: int,\r\n) -> None:\r\n    global _WORKER_TEST_OBS, _WORKER_TEST_Y, _WORKER_ORACLE, _WORKER_ORACLE_BRIER\r\n    global _WORKER_PARAMS, _WORKER_POLICIES, _WORKER_LR, _WORKER_STEPS\r\n    try:\r\n        import torch\r\n    except ImportError as exc:\r\n        raise ImportError(\"PyTorch is required to run E13 adaptation jobs\") from exc\r\n    torch.set_num_threads(1)\r\n    _WORKER_TEST_OBS = test_observations\r\n    _WORKER_TEST_Y = np.asarray(test_y)\r\n    _WORKER_ORACLE = oracle_predictions\r\n    _WORKER_ORACLE_BRIER = {\r\n        policy: float(np.mean((prediction - _WORKER_TEST_Y) ** 2))\r\n        for policy, prediction in oracle_predictions.items()\r\n    }\r\n    _WORKER_PARAMS = e12._load_parameters(params_path)\r\n    _WORKER_POLICIES = e12._policies()\r\n    _WORKER_LR = float(lr)\r\n    _WORKER_STEPS = int(steps)\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _run_job(task: Mapping[str, object]) -> Dict[str, object]:\r\n    if _WORKER_TEST_Y is None:\r\n        raise RuntimeError(\"E13 worker was not initialized\")\r\n    started = time.perf_counter()\r\n    policy_name = str(task[\"policy\"])\r\n    policy_index = int(task[\"policy_index\"])\r\n    draw = int(task[\"draw\"])\r\n    week = int(task[\"week\"])\r\n    n_patients = N_PER_WEEK * week\r\n    latent_seed, observation_seed, batch_seed = _stream_seeds(policy_index, draw)\r\n    stream_latent = sim.simulate_latent(STREAM_N, seed=latent_seed)\r\n    stream_all = sim.observe(\r\n        stream_latent,\r\n        _WORKER_POLICIES[policy_name],\r\n        sigma=SIGMA,\r\n        seed=observation_seed,\r\n    )\r\n    stream = {\r\n        \"x\": np.asarray(stream_all[\"x\"][:n_patients], dtype=np.float32),\r\n        \"m\": np.asarray(stream_all[\"m\"][:n_patients], dtype=np.int8),\r\n    }\r\n    stream_y = np.asarray(stream_latent[\"y\"][:n_patients], dtype=np.float32)\r\n    n_cases = int(stream_y.sum())\r\n    test_observed = _WORKER_TEST_OBS[policy_name]\r\n    test_y = _WORKER_TEST_Y\r\n    oracle_brier = _WORKER_ORACLE_BRIER[policy_name]\r\n    params = _WORKER_PARAMS\r\n    rows: List[Dict[str, object]] = []\r\n\r\n    # R1 and R2 share the same refitted testing head. Its fit is unlabelled.\r\n    method_started = time.perf_counter()\r\n    head_u_params = e12._refit_testing_head(\r\n        stream,\r\n        None,\r\n        params,\r\n        batch_seed=batch_seed,\r\n        steps=_WORKER_STEPS,\r\n        lr=_WORKER_LR,\r\n    )\r\n    head_u_prediction, _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(\r\n        test_observed[\"x\"], test_observed[\"m\"], head_u_params, w=1.0\r\n    )\r\n    rows.append({\r\n        \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n        \"method\": \"head_U\",\r\n        \"week\": week,\r\n        \"n_patients\": n_patients,\r\n        \"n_cases\": 0,\r\n        \"draw\": draw,\r\n        **e12._metrics(test_y, head_u_prediction, oracle_brier),\r\n        \"w\": 1.0,\r\n        \"wall_time_seconds\": time.perf_counter() - method_started,\r\n    })\r\n\r\n    method_started = time.perf_counter()\r\n    head_u_dial_w = _choose_penalized_weight(\r\n        stream[\"x\"], stream[\"m\"], stream_y, head_u_params\r\n    )\r\n    head_u_dial_prediction, _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(\r\n        test_observed[\"x\"], test_observed[\"m\"], head_u_params, w=head_u_dial_w\r\n    )\r\n    rows.append({\r\n        \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n        \"method\": \"head_U_dial\",\r\n        \"week\": week,\r\n        \"n_patients\": n_patients,\r\n        \"n_cases\": n_cases,\r\n        \"draw\": draw,\r\n        **e12._metrics(test_y, head_u_dial_prediction, oracle_brier),\r\n        \"w\": head_u_dial_w,\r\n        \"wall_time_seconds\": time.perf_counter() - method_started,\r\n    })\r\n\r\n    method_started = time.perf_counter()\r\n    dial_only_w = _choose_penalized_weight(\r\n        stream[\"x\"], stream[\"m\"], stream_y, params\r\n    )\r\n    dial_only_prediction, _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(\r\n        test_observed[\"x\"], test_observed[\"m\"], params, w=dial_only_w\r\n    )\r\n    rows.append({\r\n        \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n        \"method\": \"dial_only\",\r\n        \"week\": week,\r\n        \"n_patients\": n_patients,\r\n        \"n_cases\": n_cases,\r\n        \"draw\": draw,\r\n        **e12._metrics(test_y, dial_only_prediction, oracle_brier),\r\n        \"w\": dial_only_w,\r\n        \"wall_time_seconds\": time.perf_counter() - method_started,\r\n    })\r\n\r\n    method_started = time.perf_counter()\r\n    finetuned_params = _finetune(\r\n        stream,\r\n        stream_y,\r\n        params,\r\n        batch_seed=batch_seed + 17,\r\n        steps=_WORKER_STEPS,\r\n        lr=_WORKER_LR,\r\n    )\r\n    finetune_prediction, _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(\r\n        test_observed[\"x\"], test_observed[\"m\"], finetuned_params, w=1.0\r\n    )\r\n    rows.append({\r\n        \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n        \"method\": \"finetune\",\r\n        \"week\": week,\r\n        \"n_patients\": n_patients,\r\n        \"n_cases\": n_cases,\r\n        \"draw\": draw,\r\n        **e12._metrics(test_y, finetune_prediction, oracle_brier),\r\n        \"w\": 1.0,\r\n        \"wall_time_seconds\": time.perf_counter() - method_started,\r\n    })\r\n\r\n    return {\r\n        \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n        \"week\": week,\r\n        \"draw\": draw,\r\n        \"rows\": rows,\r\n        \"elapsed\": time.perf_counter() - started,\r\n    }\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _make_test_cohort(n_test: int):\r\n    policies = e12._policies()\r\n    test_latent = sim.simulate_latent(n_test, seed=TEST_LATENT_SEED)\r\n    test_y = np.asarray(test_latent[\"y\"], dtype=np.float32)\r\n    test_observations: Dict[str, Mapping[str, np.ndarray]] = {}\r\n    oracle_predictions: Dict[str, np.ndarray] = {}\r\n    for policy_index, policy_name in enumerate(POLICY_NAMES):\r\n        policy = policies[policy_name]\r\n        observed = sim.observe(\r\n            test_latent,\r\n            policy,\r\n            sigma=SIGMA,\r\n            seed=TEST_OBS_SEED_BASE + policy_index,\r\n        )\r\n        test_observations[policy_name] = observed\r\n        oracle_predictions[policy_name] = bayes.bayes_predict(\r\n            observed[\"x\"], observed[\"m\"], SIGMA, policy\r\n        )\r\n    return policies, test_observations, test_y, oracle_predictions\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _summary_frame(rows: Sequence[Mapping[str, object]]) -> pd.DataFrame:\r\n    frame = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=RUN_COLUMNS)\r\n    grouped = frame.groupby([\"policy\", \"method\", \"week\"], sort=False)[\r\n        list(SUMMARY_METRICS)\r\n    ].agg([\"mean\", \"min\", \"max\"])\r\n    grouped.columns = [f\"{column}_{stat}\" for column, stat in grouped.columns]\r\n    return grouped.reset_index()\r\n\r\n\r\ndef run(\r\n    *,\r\n    smoke: bool = False,\r\n    workers: int = 4,\r\n    lr: float = 0.05,\r\n    steps: int = 160,\r\n    train_only: bool = False,\r\n) -> Tuple[Path | None, Path | None]:\r\n    if workers < 1:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"--workers must be at least 1\")\r\n    if steps < 1:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"--steps must be at least 1\")\r\n    if lr <= 0:\r\n        raise ValueError(\"--lr must be positive\")\r\n\r\n    params_path, _ = _train_or_load_a_model(smoke)\r\n    if train_only:\r\n        return None, None\r\n\r\n    n_test = 500 if smoke else 10_000\r\n    weeks = SMOKE_WEEKS if smoke else WEEKS\r\n    draws = 1 if smoke else 5\r\n    policies, test_observations, test_y, oracle_predictions = _make_test_cohort(n_test)\r\n    params = e12._load_parameters(params_path)\r\n    oracle_brier = {\r\n        name: float(np.mean((oracle_predictions[name] - test_y) ** 2))\r\n        for name in POLICY_NAMES\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    # Compute static R0 and oracle predictions once per policy. A copy of each\r\n    # metric row is placed at every week so the summary and figure have flat\r\n    # reference curves on the same x axis as the adapting methods.\r\n    static_rows: List[Dict[str, object]] = []\r\n    for policy_name in POLICY_NAMES:\r\n        observed = test_observations[policy_name]\r\n        keep_prediction, _ = latent_filter.numpy_forward(\r\n            observed[\"x\"], observed[\"m\"], params, w=1.0\r\n        )\r\n        for week in weeks:\r\n            n_patients = N_PER_WEEK * week\r\n            static_rows.append({\r\n                \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n                \"method\": \"keep\",\r\n                \"week\": week,\r\n                \"n_patients\": n_patients,\r\n                \"n_cases\": 0,\r\n                \"draw\": 0,\r\n                **e12._metrics(test_y, keep_prediction, oracle_brier[policy_name]),\r\n                \"w\": 1.0,\r\n                \"wall_time_seconds\": 0.0,\r\n            })\r\n            static_rows.append({\r\n                \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n                \"method\": \"oracle\",\r\n                \"week\": week,\r\n                \"n_patients\": n_patients,\r\n                \"n_cases\": 0,\r\n                \"draw\": 0,\r\n                **e12._metrics(\r\n                    test_y, oracle_predictions[policy_name], oracle_brier[policy_name]\r\n                ),\r\n                \"w\": float(\"nan\"),\r\n                \"wall_time_seconds\": 0.0,\r\n            })\r\n\r\n    tasks = [\r\n        {\r\n            \"policy\": policy_name,\r\n            \"policy_index\": policy_index,\r\n            \"draw\": draw,\r\n            \"week\": week,\r\n        }\r\n        for policy_index, policy_name in enumerate(POLICY_NAMES)\r\n        for draw in range(draws)\r\n        for week in weeks\r\n    ]\r\n    job_rows: List[Dict[str, object]] = []\r\n    context = mp.get_context(\"spawn\")\r\n    with context.Pool(\r\n        processes=workers,\r\n        initializer=_init_worker,\r\n        initargs=(\r\n            test_observations,\r\n            test_y,\r\n            oracle_predictions,\r\n            str(params_path),\r\n            lr,\r\n            2 if smoke else steps,\r\n        ),\r\n    ) as pool:\r\n        for result in pool.imap_unordered(_run_job, tasks, chunksize=1):\r\n            job_rows.extend(result[\"rows\"])\r\n            print(\r\n                f\"DONE {result['policy']} draw={result['draw']} \"\r\n                f\"week={result['week']} wall={result['elapsed']:.1f}s\",\r\n                flush=True,\r\n            )\r\n\r\n    rows = static_rows + job_rows\r\n    analysis_dir = ROOT / \"analysis\"\r\n    analysis_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\r\n    prefix = \"e13_smoke\" if smoke else \"e13\"\r\n    runs_path = analysis_dir / f\"{prefix}_runs.csv\"\r\n    summary_path = analysis_dir / f\"{prefix}_summary.csv\"\r\n    runs_frame = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=RUN_COLUMNS)\r\n    policy_order = {name: index for index, name in enumerate(POLICY_NAMES)}\r\n    method_order = {\r\n        \"keep\": 0, \"head_U\": 1, \"head_U_dial\": 2, \"finetune\": 3,\r\n        \"dial_only\": 4, \"oracle\": 5,\r\n    }\r\n    runs_frame[\"_policy_order\"] = runs_frame[\"policy\"].map(policy_order)\r\n    runs_frame[\"_method_order\"] = runs_frame[\"method\"].map(method_order)\r\n    runs_frame.sort_values(\r\n        [\"_policy_order\", \"week\", \"_method_order\", \"draw\"], inplace=True\r\n    )\r\n    runs_frame.drop(columns=[\"_policy_order\", \"_method_order\"]).to_csv(\r\n        runs_path, index=False\r\n    )\r\n    _summary_frame(rows).to_csv(summary_path, index=False)\r\n    print(f\"WROTE {runs_path}\", flush=True)\r\n    print(f\"WROTE {summary_path}\", flush=True)\r\n    return runs_path, summary_path\r\n\r\n\r\ndef main(argv: Iterable[str] | None = None) -> None:\r\n    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__)\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--smoke\", action=\"store_true\")\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--workers\", type=int, default=4)\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--lr\", type=float, default=0.05)\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--steps\", type=int, default=160)\r\n    parser.add_argument(\"--train-only\", action=\"store_true\")\r\n    args = parser.parse_args(argv)\r\n    run(\r\n        smoke=args.smoke,\r\n        workers=args.workers,\r\n        lr=args.lr,\r\n        steps=args.steps,\r\n        train_only=args.train_only,\r\n    )\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    mp.freeze_support()\r\n    main()\r\n\"\"\"Plot the E13 regret curves from ``analysis/e13_summary.csv``.\"\"\"\r\n\r\nfrom __future__ import annotations\r\n\r\nimport csv\r\nfrom pathlib import Path\r\nfrom typing import Dict, Iterable, Mapping, Sequence, Tuple\r\n\r\n\r\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\r\nSUMMARY_PATH = ROOT / \"analysis\" / \"e13_summary.csv\"\r\nFIGURE_DIR = ROOT / \"figures\"\r\nWEEKS = (1, 2, 4, 8, 13, 26, 52, 104)\r\nPOLICY_TITLES = {\r\n    \"B_less\": \"B less selective\",\r\n    \"B_more\": \"B more selective\",\r\n    \"B_match\": \"B tests at random (same rate)\",\r\n    \"B_dense\": \"B tests at random (twice as often)\",\r\n}\r\nMETHODS = (\"keep\", \"dial_only\", \"head_U\", \"head_U_dial\", \"finetune\")\r\nCOLORS = {\r\n    \"keep\": \"#666666\",\r\n    \"dial_only\": \"#0072B2\",\r\n    \"head_U\": \"#D55E00\",\r\n    \"head_U_dial\": \"#CC79A7\",\r\n    \"finetune\": \"#009E73\",\r\n}\r\nLABELS = {\r\n    \"keep\": \"keep (A model)\",\r\n    \"dial_only\": \"dial only\",\r\n    \"head_U\": \"head_U\",\r\n    \"head_U_dial\": \"head_U + dial\",\r\n    \"finetune\": \"fine-tune all parameters\",\r\n}\r\nMARKERS = {\"keep\": \"o\", \"dial_only\": \"s\", \"head_U\": \"^\", \"head_U_dial\": \"D\", \"finetune\": \"P\"}\r\n\r\n\r\ndef _read_summary(path: Path) -> Dict[Tuple[str, str, int], Mapping[str, str]]:\r\n    with path.open(\"r\", newline=\"\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle:\r\n        rows = csv.DictReader(handle)\r\n        return {\r\n            (row[\"policy\"], row[\"method\"], int(row[\"week\"])): row\r\n            for row in rows\r\n        }\r\n\r\n\r\ndef make_figure(summary_path: Path = SUMMARY_PATH) -> Tuple[Path, Path]:\r\n    import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\r\n\r\n    data = _read_summary(summary_path)\r\n    FIGURE_DIR.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\r\n    fig, axes = plt.subplots(2, 2, figsize=(11.2, 7.7), sharex=True, sharey=True)\r\n    axes_flat = axes.ravel()\r\n    label_handles = {}\r\n\r\n    for ax, policy_name in zip(axes_flat, POLICY_TITLES):\r\n        policy_rows = {\r\n            (method, week): data[(policy_name, method, week)]\r\n            for method in (*METHODS, \"oracle\")\r\n            for week in WEEKS\r\n            if (policy_name, method, week) in data\r\n        }\r\n        case_counts = []\r\n        for week in WEEKS:\r\n            row = policy_rows.get((\"dial_only\", week))\r\n            case_counts.append(float(row[\"n_cases_mean\"]) if row else float(\"nan\"))\r\n\r\n        x = list(WEEKS)\r\n        # No best-method shading: head_U and head_U + dial are tied to 1e-5,\r\n        # so the shading flipped between them on noise (23 Oct 2026).\r\n\r\n        for method in METHODS:\r\n            method_rows = [policy_rows.get((method, week)) for week in WEEKS]\r\n            if not all(method_rows):\r\n                continue\r\n            means = [float(row[\"regret_mean\"]) for row in method_rows]\r\n            lows = [float(row[\"regret_min\"]) for row in method_rows]\r\n            highs = [float(row[\"regret_max\"]) for row in method_rows]\r\n            linestyle = \"--\" if method == \"keep\" else \"-\"\r\n            line, = ax.plot(\r\n                x,\r\n                means,\r\n                color=COLORS[method],\r\n                marker=MARKERS[method],\r\n                markersize=4.5,\r\n                linewidth=1.8,\r\n                linestyle=linestyle,\r\n                label=LABELS[method],\r\n                zorder=3,\r\n            )\r\n            ax.fill_between(\r\n                x,\r\n                lows,\r\n                highs,\r\n                color=COLORS[method],\r\n                alpha=0.14,\r\n                linewidth=0,\r\n                zorder=2,\r\n            )\r\n            label_handles[method] = line\r\n\r\n        tick_labels = [\r\n            f\"{week}\\n{count:.1f}\" if count == count else str(week)\r\n            for week, count in zip(WEEKS, case_counts)\r\n        ]\r\n        ax.set_xscale(\"log\")\r\n        # Log scale: fine-tuning regret is 10-50x the others at small run-ins.\r\n        ax.set_yscale(\"log\")\r\n        ax.set_xticks(WEEKS)\r\n        ax.set_xticklabels(tick_labels)\r\n        ax.xaxis.set_minor_locator(plt.NullLocator())\r\n        ax.tick_params(axis=\"x\", labelbottom=True)\r\n        ax.set_title(POLICY_TITLES[policy_name], fontsize=10, pad=8)\r\n        ax.grid(axis=\"y\", color=\"#d9d9d9\", linewidth=0.7, zorder=1)\r\n        ax.set_axisbelow(True)\r\n\r\n    axes_flat[0].set_ylabel(\"Regret (Brier minus B optimum)\")\r\n    axes_flat[2].set_ylabel(\"Regret (Brier minus B optimum)\")\r\n    for ax in (axes_flat[2], axes_flat[3]):\r\n        ax.set_xlabel(\"Weeks since deployment\\nMean labelled outcome cases\")\r\n    for ax in (axes_flat[0], axes_flat[1]):\r\n        ax.set_xlabel(\"Weeks since deployment\\nMean labelled outcome cases\")\r\n\r\n    handles = [label_handles[method] for method in METHODS if method in label_handles]\r\n    fig.legend(\r\n        handles,\r\n        [LABELS[method] for method in METHODS if method in label_handles],\r\n        loc=\"lower center\",\r\n        ncol=5,\r\n        frameon=False,\r\n        bbox_to_anchor=(0.5, 0.015),\r\n        fontsize=9,\r\n    )\r\n    fig.tight_layout(rect=(0, 0.10, 1, 1), h_pad=1.5, w_pad=1.4)\r\n    png_path = FIGURE_DIR / \"e13_rungs_over_time.png\"\r\n    pdf_path = FIGURE_DIR / \"e13_rungs_over_time.pdf\"\r\n    fig.savefig(png_path, dpi=300, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\r\n    fig.savefig(pdf_path, bbox_inches=\"tight\")\r\n    plt.close(fig)\r\n    return png_path, pdf_path\r\n\r\n\r\ndef main(argv: Iterable[str] | None = None) -> None:\r\n    del argv\r\n    png_path, pdf_path = make_figure()\r\n    print(f\"WROTE {png_path}\")\r\n    print(f\"WROTE {pdf_path}\")\r\n\r\n\r\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\r\n    main()\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
