{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\WINDOWS\\\\System32\\\\WindowsPowerShell\\\\v1.0\\\\powershell.exe\" -Command '$p='\"'SPEC.md'; \"'$a=Get-Content $p; for($i=195;$i -lt [Math]::Min($a.Length,290);$i++){ '\"'{0,4}: {1}' -f (\"'$i+1),$a[$i] };'","aggregated_output":" 196: ## E12: re-learning the testing head at B without outcomes (Claude and Wesley, 23 Oct 2026; exploratory, quick version)\r\n 197: \r\n 198: Question (from a teammate, 22 Oct): rung 3 re-learns only the testing head. The testing head is a likelihood of test orders given the latent state, so it can be fitted on B's records without outcome labels, which exist for every patient. How does that compare with the labelled rungs, as a function of how much data each uses?\r\n 199: \r\n 200: Model: the E5 filter already trained at A (`results/E5/sigma1_gamma2/seed0/filter/params.npz`, sigma 1, A = informative gamma 2, rate 0.3). No retraining at A.\r\n 201: \r\n 202: Target policies at B (sigma 1, rate 0.3 unless stated): `B_less` informative gamma 1 (less selective); `B_more` informative gamma 3.5 (more selective; the dial in [0, 1] cannot help here, see docs/GAP.md \"The dial above 1\"); `B_match` uniform 0.3; `B_dense` uniform 0.6.\r\n 203: \r\n 204: Data at B: test cohort n = 8,000 (E5 test latent seed, new observation seed per policy); labelled run-in pools and unlabelled pools from new latent seeds, disjoint from the test cohort. Outcome prevalence is about 16%.\r\n 205: \r\n 206: Methods (all start from the A parameters; only the testing-head logits c, 32 numbers, or w change):\r\n 207: - `keep`: w = 1. `drop`: w = 0.\r\n 208: - `dial_L` (rung 1): w on the grid 0, 0.05, ..., 1 minimising log loss on the labelled run-in (ties to 1).\r\n 209: - `head_L` (rung 3, labelled): refit c on the labelled run-in with the E5 training loss (BCE + per-step NLL of values and masks), all other parameters frozen.\r\n 210: - `head_U` (rung 3, no outcomes): refit c on an unlabelled pool by the per-step NLL of values and masks only, all other parameters frozen.\r\n 211: - `oracle`: exact Bayes predictor under the B policy (`bayes.bayes_predict`).\r\n 212: Refits: Adam lr 1e-2, minibatch 256, 80 steps, initialised at A's c; no early stopping.\r\n 213: \r\n 214: Sizes: labelled run-in n_L = 30, 125, 500 patients (about 5, 20, 80 outcome cases); unlabelled pool n_U = 200, 2,000 patients. 3 draws per size and policy. Quick version; more draws only if the answer is unclear.\r\n 215: \r\n 216: Outputs: per policy, method, size and draw: Brier, log loss, AUROC on the B test cohort, and regret = Brier minus oracle Brier; the refitted c curves for `head_U` at n_U = 2,000 against the true B testing curve on the latent grid (sanity check; states need not align, see E5). Files `analysis/e12_runs.csv`, `analysis/e12_summary.csv`, `analysis/e12_c_curves.csv`.\r\n 217: \r\n 218: Expectation, stated in advance: in this simulator testing depends only on the latent state, so B's change of policy is the case `head_U` is built for; it should approach the oracle without labels, and handle `B_more`, where the dial cannot. A shift in what a test means for the outcome beyond the state cannot be simulated here, and `head_U` would not detect it.\r\n 219: \r\n 220: ## E13: which rung when? Performance over weeks of deployment (Claude and Wesley, 23 Oct 2026; simulation)\r\n 221: \r\n 222: Question: as data from hospital B accrue week by week after deployment, which rung of the ladder is best at each time, and where do the curves cross? Main figure: x = weeks since deployment (log scale), y = regret against the exact optimum at B, one curve per rung, the best rung per week shaded.\r\n 223: \r\n 224: Ladder (ordered by outcome labels needed; Wesley, 23 Oct; docs/ARGUMENT.md section 10):\r\n 225: - R0 `keep`: A's model.\r\n 226: - R1 `head_U`: re-learn the testing-head logits c on all of B's records so far, without outcomes (value-and-mask likelihood only), other parameters frozen.\r\n 227: - R2 `head_U_dial`: R1, then one weight w in [0, 1] on the re-learned testing evidence, chosen on the labelled patients so far by penalised log loss: sum of log losses + 2 * (w - 1)^2 (E9's prior, sd 0.5 around 1); no outcome case yet -> w = 1.\r\n 228: - R4 `finetune`: all parameters from A's values, on the labelled patients so far, loss BCE + per-step NLL (E5 loss); no outcome case yet -> A's model.\r\n 229: - Baseline `dial_only`: the original dial on A's model (no re-learning), same penalised rule as R2.\r\n 230: - R3 (dial per test group) needs several tests; the simulator has one. Left for the multi-test simulator.\r\n 231: - `oracle`: exact Bayes predictor under B's policy.\r\n 232: \r\n 233: Realistic rates: outcome threshold raised from 1.0 to 2.05 so that about 2% of patients have the outcome (as sepsis in E6). This changes the A model: retrain the E5 filter at A (informative gamma 2, rate 0.3, sigma 1) with this outcome, n_train 16,000, n_val 4,000 (about 320 and 80 cases), otherwise as E5 (`latent_filter.train_filter`). Implementation: set `sim.THRESHOLD` and `bayes.THRESHOLD` (bayes imports it by value) to 2.05 at import time of the E13 script, so spawned workers see it too.\r\n 234: \r\n 235: Time axis: weeks t = 1, 2, 4, 8, 13, 26, 52, 104; 25 admissions per week (1,300 a year, a median US ICU), so n(t) = 25 t patients (about 0.5 outcome cases a week). All n(t) patients' testing records are available for R1; their outcomes are available for R2, R4 and `dial_only` (outcome delay ignored: the 6-hour horizon is short against a week).\r\n 236: \r\n 237: B policies: as E12 (`B_less`, `B_more`, `B_match`, `B_dense`). Test cohort 10,000 patients (about 200 cases), fixed. 5 draws of the deployment stream per week and policy; the stream is nested (week t's patients are the first 25 t of one stream of 2,600 per draw), so curves over weeks are paired.\r\n 238: \r\n 239: Refits (R1 and R4): Adam, minibatch 256 with replacement, settings from E12b if it converges (lr 0.05, 160 steps), else stated with the result.\r\n 240: \r\n 241: Outputs: `analysis/e13_runs.csv` (policy, method, week, n patients, n cases, draw, Brier, log loss, AUROC, regret, w), `analysis/e13_summary.csv`, `figures/e13_rungs_over_time.png` (one panel per policy, mean regret with min-max band over draws, shading of the best rung per week).\r\n 242: \r\n 243: Expectation, stated in advance: R1 is best from the first week when B's testing still depends on the state; R2 adds little here, because the simulator shifts only testing (its job is a shift in a test's meaning for the outcome, which cannot be simulated with this simulator); R4 overtakes only after many months, if at all within two years. A rule that chooses the rung from run-in data alone is the next step, not part of E13.\r\n 244: \r\n 245: ## E14: a shift only outcomes can reveal (Claude and Wesley, 25 Oct 2026; simulation, quick version)\r\n 246: \r\n 247: Question: E12-E13 shifted only how testing depends on the patient's state, which B's unlabelled records reveal, and head_U won at every run-in length. Is there a shift that head_U cannot fix, where the outcome-based rungs earn their place, and does head_U at least do no harm there? This turns \"which rung\" into \"which kind of shift\", and E14 checks that reading.\r\n 248: \r\n 249: Simulator extension (new module `sim_u.py`, `sim.py` and `bayes.py` unchanged): each patient has a hidden binary trait u ~ Bernoulli(0.3), independent of the AR(1) path z (think: a risk factor the doctor sees at the bedside but the model does not, e.g. frailty or a worrying history). The outcome uses it at both hospitals: y = 1[z_future + 1.0 * u > threshold], threshold chosen so that about 2% of patients have the outcome (solve by quadrature, assert 1-3% on 20,000 patients). Testing: P(test at t) = sigmoid(alpha + gamma * z_t + beta * u), alpha set so the marginal testing rate is 0.3 (z ~ N(0, 1) stationary, u mixture); values as before, sigma 1. Policy \"u-informative\" with parameters (gamma, beta).\r\n 250: \r\n 251: Two directions:\r\n 252: - `gain`: A tests on the state only (gamma 2, beta 0); B's doctors also test on the trait (gamma 2, beta 2). At B a test means more for the outcome than at A. B's records alone show that testing varies between patients more than the state explains, but not that this extra testing predicts the outcome. Expected: head_U cannot exploit it (A's states do not represent u, and the outcome head is frozen); rungs with outcomes can, given enough cases.\r\n 253: - `lose`: A tests on both (gamma 2, beta 2); B on the state only (gamma 2, beta 0). At B a test means less. Expected: the A model learns states that carry u (persistent, from the testing pattern); head_U re-learns that testing at B no longer differs between these states and so stops reading u into tests, without outcomes.\r\n 254: \r\n 255: A models: one per direction, as E13 (E5 filter, K = 32, n_train 16,000, n_val 4,000, `latent_filter.train_filter`), trained on A's policy with the new outcome; cached under `results/E14/a_model_<direction>/`.\r\n 256: \r\n 257: Oracle: exact Bayes predictor under B's policy, a mixture over u: for each u run the 241-point grid filter with alpha + beta * u, keeping the log evidence of the history; P(y | h) = sum over u of P(u | h) * P(z_future > threshold - u | h, u). Checks: with beta = 0, P(u = 1 | h) is 0.3 for every patient; with beta = 0 and the outcome shift set to 0, the predictor equals `bayes.bayes_predict` at the same threshold to 1e-10.\r\n 258: \r\n 259: Methods per (direction, draw, week), as E13 plus a labelled testing-head refit:\r\n 260: - `keep`, `head_U` (E12's `_refit_testing_head` without labels), `dial_only` (penalised w in [0, 1], E13 rule), `head_L` (E12's `_refit_testing_head` with the stream's outcomes: BCE + NLL, c only), `finetune_lr05` and `finetune_lr005` (E13's `_finetune` at lr 0.05 and 0.005), `oracle`. Refits: 160 steps, minibatch 256 with replacement, lr 0.05 unless named. Zero outcome cases: labelled methods return A's model.\r\n 261: \r\n 262: Quick design: weeks 4, 13, 52, 104 at 25 patients a week (nested streams of 2,600, as E13), 2 draws, test cohort 10,000. Outputs `analysis/e14_runs.csv`, `analysis/e14_summary.csv`, `figures/e14_meaning_shift.png` (one panel per direction, same style as the fixed E13 figure: log axes, no shading). Also report per direction the regret of `keep` and the oracle's AUROC gain over `keep`, so we know how large the shift is.\r\n 263: \r\n 264: Expectation, stated in advance: `lose`: head_U recovers most of keep's regret from week 4. `gain`: head_U close to keep (neither helps nor harms); head_L or fine-tuning gain only with many cases, if at all within 2 years. If `gain` shows no method gaining within 2 years, the reading is that a shift in what tests mean for the outcome cannot be fixed from one ICU's run-in, only detected.\r\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
